WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:09.370
- Hello everyone and good
morning, good afternoon,

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.260
or good evening, wherever
you're coming from.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.680
My name is Ato Quayson

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.000
and I'm a professor of English
at Stanford University,

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.940
and I have the very, very happy position

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.190
of moderating this magnificent session.

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:28.430
Let me start over right
away by welcoming you all,

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:33.230
but, and then also by giving
just brief introductions

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:38.090
to the various directors and the speakers.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.420
First, we have Jennifer DeVere Brody,

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.980
who is professor in theater
and performance studies

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.610
at Stanford, where she serves
as the faculty director

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:50.180
of the Center for Comparative
Studies in Race and Ethnicity.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.510
In other words, she's my colleague.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:53.643
Hi, Jennifer.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.780
Stephen Pitti is the Director

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.710
of the Center for the Study of Race,

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.830
Indigeneity and Transnational Migration

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:05.760
and a professor of history,
American Studies and Ethnicity,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.870
Race and Migration at Yale University.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:09.703
Hi, Stephen.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:15.770
C. Riley Snorton is the
interim faculty director

00:01:15.877 --> 00:01:19.890
at the Center for the Study
of Race, Politics and Culture,

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.420
and a professor of English and Literature

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.500
and Gender and Sexuality Studies

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:28.563
at the University of Chicago, hi.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.040
Tricia Rose is chancellor's
professor of Africana Studies

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:34.873
and the director of

00:01:34.873 --> 00:01:37.820
the Center for the Study
of Race and Ethnicity

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.170
in America at Brown University.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.080
Hi, Tricia.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.627
So, we open with Jennifer.

00:01:44.627 --> 00:01:46.780
So, Jennifer over to you.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.190
- Thank you so much Ato
and colleagues here.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:54.050
It is indeed an honor to
be part of this collective

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.653
and this conference on racial reckoning.

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:58.743
I'm speaking to you

00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:02.010
from the unceded Muwekma
Ohlone native homelands,

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.490
an acknowledgement that
only begins to reckon

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.750
with the reparations
required to repair the damage

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.310
of centuries of racial capitalism

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:12.300
that calls upon us to exercise

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.750
in Theresa Montoya's words,
radical vulnerability.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:19.610
In yesterday's panel,
colleagues spoke eloquently

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:22.430
about the reorganization
of knowledge, power,

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.070
Safwat hoopla needed to
transform the university.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:30.010
As the recent news about the
daughter of the African family

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.732
and Philadelphia's MOVE program

00:02:32.732 --> 00:02:35.740
whose bones were used
without consent for a lecture

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.770
involving my Alma Mater,
University of Pennsylvania

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:40.710
and Princeton University,

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:43.980
often known as the
Northern most institution

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.130
that sits on the land of the Linaila Nape,

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.090
and also where I was born,

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:54.090
has shown universities have
trafficked for many centuries

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:58.260
throughout the Americas and in Europe

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:04.130
in the bones of the ancestors.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:07.080
And so we're thinking here
about a reckoning of bones

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.040
from the Americas to Auschwitz,

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.540
to gynecologist, Marion Sims

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:13.590
and that's a shout out to Riley Snorton

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.030
who's written with such moral clarity

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.310
about Sims' transgression

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.423
with the bones of the dead and the living.

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:24.850
And so, our bodies and
our bones have been used

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.020
without consent in the name of knowledge

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:30.810
that benefits what Ferguson
yesterday talked about

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.130
as a professional exercise in benchmarks.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:37.210
Indeed, all the panelists
yesterday shared their concern,

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:40.040
which we're going to
continue talking about today,

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:43.550
about how to work more
ethically and equitably

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.980
with the punitive objects and subjects

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.150
that the university hierarchies
produce and sustain.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:51.570
And I think that really is our focus

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:55.160
on critical race studies
and its institutionalization

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.970
within the larger
understandings of knowledge.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:01.990
So, in my nearly 30 years as professor,

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.310
having taught at seven universities

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:05.880
in many different departments,

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.380
English, African, and
African-American studies,

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.100
gender and sexuality
studies, performance studies,

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.610
I have often looked to,
not only other scholars

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.700
in critical race studies,

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.840
which was really organized in the 1990s

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:23.279
by a group of critical
race and legal scholars,

00:04:23.279 --> 00:04:26.790
but I've also looked at
artists whose work intervenes

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:30.270
in the restricted
covenants of racial order.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.070
In preparing these short remarks,

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:35.260
I had too many examples
of contemporary scholars

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:38.360
and artists, black and brown,

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.810
those who work for civil
rights, feminist, art, act up,

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:44.360
AIDS, anti-war protests,

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.300
against the prison industrial complex,

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.980
indigenous rights, police
violence, farm workers unions,

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.562
disability rights, occupy trans justice,

00:04:52.562 --> 00:04:54.573
anti-nuclear protests,

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:59.080
demonstrations against the
World Trade Organization,

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.330
the bay areas, people kitchen collective,

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:02.591
I could go on and on,

00:05:02.591 --> 00:05:06.510
but to choose from people who are working

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:11.090
to "the motto" of the
people's kitchen collective

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:13.840
here in Oakland, and I
do some work with them,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.613
to feed the mind, nurse the
soul and fuel a movement.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.080
And I'm pointing to them
in part not only because

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.690
they do amazing work on
recreating, for example,

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:29.470
the free breakfast in DeFremery,
Little Bobby Hutton park

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:31.460
in homage to the Black Panthers,

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.760
but to try to get at this
idea of what kinds of care

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:39.130
or carry tasks that the
university in its Cartesian claims

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:44.037
to specific forms of rational
thought too often elides.

00:05:45.672 --> 00:05:48.290
And so I'm thinking a little
bit of Charles Mills' work here

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:51.500
on the racial contract, on
the whole sort of history

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.380
that organizes Western knowledge,

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.256
that puts those of us who are working

00:05:55.256 --> 00:05:58.200
in the margins around
critical race studies

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:03.280
and want to transform that knowledge

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.040
in a slightly oppositional
or complicated space

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.470
vis-a-vis traditional
forms of understanding

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:12.933
what counts as thought.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.980
So, these academics and
artists that I've mentioned

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:20.700
begin to try to break down
some of those barriers

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:25.040
between theory and practice
and Marxist terms of practice

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.530
and intervene in university structures

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:31.090
that institutionalize a
hierarchical division of labor

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.920
between scholars and researchers

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:34.890
or artists and practitioners.

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:37.350
Something that the
center where I work now,

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:40.240
for comparative studies in
race and ethnicity at Stanford

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:45.240
has tried to dismantle by asking
practitioners, for example,

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:48.360
working on ethics, race, and technology,

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.660
to come into the university and teach us

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:52.540
and get us to think differently

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:54.260
about the knowledge that they produce

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:57.030
in their own spaces of work.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:00.240
And similarly, in theater
performance studies,

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.900
we have tried to tenure artists.

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:06.460
So, what counts as the
book or what is tenurable

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.520
begins to be undone.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.230
And I'm trying to talk here a little bit

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:14.640
about the segregation of
faculty, not only from students,

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:18.320
but of different kinds of valuation,

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:19.250
of what counts

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:23.109
as the imaginative creative
dimension of scholarship.

00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:26.220
And again, I join a long, long list

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:28.070
where Tim Spillers has talked about this

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:29.277
in her classic essay on

00:07:29.277 --> 00:07:33.320
"Harold Cruises: The Crisis
of The Negro Intellectual,"

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:36.800
how is it that we want to
value the different subjects

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:40.190
at different ways of
knowing in the university

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:43.350
that can maybe go against
certain enlightenment legacies

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:46.340
that are race and de-value embodied work,

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.200
which is concomitant
with the mind-body split,

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.190
where disabled, working class,
female and racialized others

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:56.450
weren't necessarily seen to
be thinking human subjects.

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:57.800
And this matters

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.240
because part of the
current racial reckoning

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.360
asks us to change the
"face of the university"

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.350
and such a shift for me must
not only be quantitative,

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.290
but also qualitative.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:11.470
We must not just change the color,

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:15.540
but also the content of academic culture,

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:19.690
redo the syllabi, reorganize
academic apartheid

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:22.240
to use Dwight Conquergood sprays.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:26.690
At Stanford, we did just finally
hire two artist scholars,

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:29.960
Yung Jing Lee among them, whose
play, "Straight White Men"

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:31.290
was the first produced

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.750
by an Asian American woman on Broadway.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:34.810
And like Toni Morrison,

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:37.650
we hope she'll be the first
tenured creative artist

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:39.520
in our program.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.990
We can see that also what happens

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:46.700
to the extra diagetic,
I'll call it, labor,

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:51.210
that too many of us
scholars of color ask to do

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:54.270
on behalf of changing the
face of the university.

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.813
I'm talking about all of the uncredited,

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:02.670
and unpaid, if you will, labor
to diversify the university.

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:04.880
How does that get accounted for

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:06.970
in questions of who is valued

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:10.120
and what is valued as forms of work

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.520
under the university rubric.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:14.370
We can see that a whole-scale

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:17.300
cultural, economic and
political change is required

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:20.680
to begin to achieve racial
justice in the university.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:22.940
In my own work, I've
spoken about the difficulty

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:25.590
of having inherited a segregated academy

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:29.010
that makes thinking about
historical connections,

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:30.480
in my case, Blacks and Britain,

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.863
I think Noemie talked
about this yesterday,

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:35.600
that made that unintelligible

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.720
in the narrow confines
of academic knowledge.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:41.130
So, again, let us remember
that racial justice

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:44.810
cannot be achieved by numbers
or any one metric alone.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:45.987
As Jeff Chang says,

00:09:45.987 --> 00:09:47.470
"When you choose to fight,

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:50.610
you hope it will not only be
merely for greater numbers,

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:53.340
but rather for more power in those numbers

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:55.267
that can lead away to equity."

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:00.200
So, this is why Adrian
Davis and our colleagues

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:01.620
who just founded a center

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.500
similar to the ones
you'll be hearing about

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:08.500
from the other faculty
directors on this meeting

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.170
called the center for race,
ethnicity, and equity.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:15.180
So, it's E2 there at
Washington University,

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:17.247
and this is a new center founded in 2020

00:10:17.247 --> 00:10:21.080
and there are a lot now being
founded across the country.

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:23.520
So, our centers that
you'll hear from today

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.123
with my colleagues on the line,

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:30.230
we met first as a collective
of directors of such centers

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:32.300
and I think one question
we have for the audience

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:33.680
and we wanna discuss is,

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:37.030
what is really the value of these centers?

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:39.060
Davis notes that without students,

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:41.190
we would just be a giant think tank.

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:43.100
And I wanna just take
a moment to give credit

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:44.360
to the student activism

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:47.140
that helped to found
my center 25 years ago

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:48.380
through a hunger strike

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:52.080
and that continues with the
founding of so many of the work

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:55.020
on expanding questions
of race and ethnicity,

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:58.600
whether that's from SF state
50 years ago, to the present

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:01.950
where we continue to see new groups.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.790
I'm getting ready to close
my comments and in doing so,

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:08.850
I just wanna take a moment
from a colleague we met.

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:10.320
I think Steve was on the line,

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:13.240
all of us at University
of Washington, St. Louis

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.880
with Adrian Davis, where Chandra Ford,

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:18.120
who was the founding director of UCLA's

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:21.760
Center for the Study of Racism,
Social Justice, and Health

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:23.340
made the very important point

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:26.450
that when she established that center

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:28.350
on Indigenous People's Day,

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:31.370
she sought to integrate
critical race studies lens

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:33.500
with public health initiatives.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:36.380
And though I think most of us
here are in the humanities,

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:38.740
I wanna point to the really breadth

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:40.310
that critical race studies can bring

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:43.430
to reorganizing the entire university.

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:46.780
And to also bring up a point Chandra made

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:50.370
where she talks about combating
liberal forms of racism,

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:52.210
which can be overshadowed

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:55.760
when we focus on the
spectacular everyday violence

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.570
that we have seen, of
course, really every day.

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:01.180
And especially over the last four years,

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:02.663
if not four centuries,

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:05.570
that if we can fail to interrogate

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:09.020
the power differentials
that organize the university

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:10.530
that are seemingly benign,

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:14.240
but I think no less
pernicious in forms of racism.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:17.650
So, we've seen a lot of
university administrations

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:21.492
give a little statement
around whatever act of heinous

00:12:21.492 --> 00:12:24.390
and spectacular violence occurs,

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:26.210
but really not necessarily look

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:30.360
to how do we change those
institutions in which we exist

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:33.470
whether the police, the
prison, the hospital,

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:35.510
or the university.

00:12:35.510 --> 00:12:38.380
To be sure, it appears that
universities across the country

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:41.860
are grappling with these
legacies of displacement

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:46.260
of enslavement and the
nefarious forms of profit,

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:47.960
which have brought them into existence.

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:49.440
And we can think about Ruth Simmons,

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:51.670
the first black woman, president of Brown,

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:53.930
leading the charge to try to think

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:56.942
about reckoning and recompense, sorry,

00:12:56.942 --> 00:13:01.942
for enslavement and indigenous
profits at the university,

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:07.480
or even at Stanford, we've
just taken down the sculpture

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:10.140
of our first president,
David Starr Jordan,

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:11.650
who was a eugenicist

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:14.900
who took part in the
racial betterment society,

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:17.570
but the renaming and the removal

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:19.470
of such vexed figures of violence

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:24.420
still leaves us in our centers
vulnerable to displacement,

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:25.810
troubled by remainders

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:29.050
and yearning for greater
equity across differences.

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:32.416
I hope that these opening remarks

00:13:32.416 --> 00:13:34.800
that draw from critical race theory,

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.420
interdisciplinary studies,

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.910
feminist studies, black
queer studies, and the like,

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:41.280
all of my disciplinary formations

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:43.510
help us to sound a call to action,

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:46.280
to mobilize compensatory redress,

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:50.360
to respond to precarious
citizens past and present,

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:53.920
and to reckon in the bones
with racial injustice.

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:55.202
Thank you.

00:13:55.202 --> 00:13:56.714
- [Ato] Thank you very much, Jennifer.

00:13:56.714 --> 00:13:57.547
Thank you so much.

00:13:57.547 --> 00:14:00.740
So, Stephen, you go next.

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:01.840
- Thank you so much, Jennifer.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:03.430
Thank you for your leadership.

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:06.600
I just admire everything
you've done there so much

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:10.580
in recent years and as
somebody who has been shaped

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:12.180
in some way by the center you run,

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:14.040
I'm just grateful for your vision

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:16.330
that you've brought to
the place in recent years.

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:18.480
So, greetings everybody from New Haven,

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:20.009
the historical and contemporary home

00:14:20.009 --> 00:14:22.120
of many indigenous peoples and nations,

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:24.447
including the Mohegan,
the Mashantucket Pequot,

00:14:24.447 --> 00:14:26.880
the Eastern Pequot, the Scatter Koch,

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:28.250
the Golden Hill Paugussett, the Niantic,

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:31.130
the Quinnipiac and others where we

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:33.840
in the Yale Center for The
Study of Race Indigeneity

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:36.040
and Transnational migration try to work

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:38.870
mindful of their presence
in its many forms,

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:41.140
contemporary and in the past.

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:43.530
I'm gonna speak in a little
bit of a different mode

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:45.540
than Jennifer just offered

00:14:46.667 --> 00:14:49.310
and talk a little bit about
the specifics of our center

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:51.853
as a way to get part of
this conversation rolling.

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:56.290
I direct the youngest center
represented here on the panel.

00:14:56.290 --> 00:15:01.290
Since we founded RITM at
Yale in the winter of 2016

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:03.900
and have just completed
our fifth anniversary,

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:07.420
the other centers represented
here are longer in the tooth,

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:09.400
more established than that.

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:11.940
Being established in the second
decade of the 20th century,

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:13.660
as we were, gave us at Yale

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:15.160
the opportunity to think for a while

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:18.870
about what an academic unit
we actually wanted to create

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:21.560
that institutional
contract that Rod Ferguson

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:23.180
talked about yesterday.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:26.210
And we had some luxury in
building on the great work

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:28.060
already happening at places like Stanford,

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:29.440
Chicago, and Brown,

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:31.840
and being able to alert our administration

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:35.040
as we made the effort to create a center

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:37.560
that they needed to enable our work

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:40.350
in establishing a race study center here

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:44.380
in order to keep step with
my colleagues on this panel

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:47.540
and their places in so many other places.

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:49.440
So, as with every other comparable center

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:50.600
I've ever heard about,

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:54.060
Yale center was a product
of political pressure

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:55.590
as well as hard intellectual work

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:59.260
and compromises with and
by the administration.

00:15:59.260 --> 00:16:01.140
So, here's our story,

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:03.130
a small group of faculty
members on our campus,

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:06.830
including me, first began
to meet and develop the idea

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:11.300
of what became the RITM center in 2007,

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:15.060
roughly eight years before
RITM was in fact established.

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:16.970
Those faculty were in departments like

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:19.980
African-American studies,
American studies, history,

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:21.930
women, gender and sexuality studies

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:24.500
and our ethnicity, race
and migration program.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:26.950
No real surprise that
those were represented.

00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:29.870
All of us were faculty of
color, are faculty of color.

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:32.510
All of us were cross
pointed in multiple units

00:16:32.510 --> 00:16:34.220
and had interdisciplinary interests

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:36.140
and struggled to meet the needs

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:38.753
and the expectations of our various units.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:43.150
We were frustrated by
failures to successfully hire,

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:45.380
recruit, and retain faculty members

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:49.170
working in fields like Latinx
studies, black studies,

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:52.470
native and indigenous studies
and Asian American studies.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:56.060
We all felt that students
interested in those fields at Yale

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:58.860
were intellectually
underserved by our university

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:00.420
in the 21st century.

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:03.930
We all felt anger about the
paucity of faculty of color

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:05.710
on our campus generally.

00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:07.670
So, in short, we were unhappy.

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:09.730
We'd watched as valued
friends and colleagues

00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:13.140
were not promoted or left our campus

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:15.220
for other seemingly more hospitable

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:16.890
colleges and universities.

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.860
And we felt tired in the 21st century,

00:17:18.860 --> 00:17:21.580
tokenized on too many committees,

00:17:21.580 --> 00:17:25.380
trotted out sometimes as
showpieces when convenient,

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:26.710
conscious that our students

00:17:26.710 --> 00:17:29.880
were not receiving the education
that they wanted and needed

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:33.300
and trapped too often in
debates with our administration

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:35.090
that we could not escape.

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:38.754
So, that was the situation
around 2007, 2008.

00:17:38.754 --> 00:17:39.940
So, that's still somewhat familiar today,

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:42.400
I'm sure to many people in this meeting,

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:43.950
and it was the situation that led us

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:46.050
to ask for a research and teaching center

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:49.070
that would both first
dignify studies of race,

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:50.740
and intellectual engagements

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.880
with core issues in ethnic
studies and related fields

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:57.230
as topics of studies, but
also as prisms and parameters

00:17:57.230 --> 00:17:58.790
for approaching these topics.

00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:02.150
And secondly, a place that would
provide resources and space

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:06.070
and a hub, perhaps a catalyst
to advance the interests

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:08.980
of members of our communities
and serve as a magnet

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:10.970
for attracting and retaining people

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:12.950
interested in race studies.

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:15.900
So, the second point is important
because we felt fractured

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:17.940
and divided and isolated

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:20.750
in separate departmental
spaces on our campus.

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:21.600
And we also knew

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:24.920
that there were just far too
few of us doing the work.

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:27.070
We were then able to
actually create the center

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:29.810
in the 2015/2016 academic year,

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:33.820
when Yale undergraduates
pushed our administration

00:18:33.820 --> 00:18:38.470
to address campus climate
and faculty hiring

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:41.040
and our undergraduate curriculum.

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:43.730
We, faculty members had
this center proposal

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:46.510
that had been sitting
collecting dust in our offices

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:48.200
from a few years before,

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:52.260
and we were already ready
to make that suggestion.

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.120
So, we took the center
proposal to our president

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.920
and our provost who
approved the general plan.

00:18:56.920 --> 00:18:58.830
But I must underscore what Jennifer said,

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:01.490
it would not have happened were
it not for student struggle

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:03.050
and student support.

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:06.020
In my final minute or two,
let me just close quickly

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:10.080
with a few notes about our
focus in the RITM center

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:13.150
and some of our struggles,
just to put these on the table.

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:15.600
First, you should know
that we've focus today

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:16.870
more on graduate students

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:19.030
than on our undergraduates on our campus

00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:21.120
as a research and teaching center.

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:24.550
While undergraduates, it
seems to us still need much

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:25.540
that they're not getting,

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:28.530
by comparison, graduate
students have had far less

00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:32.170
structured support on our
campus as emerging scholars.

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:35.370
And because we in the center
wanted to shape research,

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:38.480
we've therefore put the
emphasis in terms of our funding

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:41.790
and our programming more
on MA and PhD thesis

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:44.190
and related projects, major projects

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:48.150
than on the support of
undergraduate classroom exercises.

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:52.670
Second, in RITM, as at Stanford,

00:19:52.670 --> 00:19:55.300
we've tried to work across
the entire university

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:57.200
throughout our professional schools,

00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:00.510
as well as in our faculty
of arts and sciences,

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:03.420
but also in our university
museums and in our theaters

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:05.560
and in another parts of our institution.

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:07.020
This has been rewarding,

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:09.650
but as you would imagine,
also challenging,

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:13.290
and that's worth talking
about, the scope and scale

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:15.180
and how to get that right.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:20.180
Third, we've tried in our
work to amplify ongoing work

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:22.940
at the university in various
departments and units

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:24.790
while also developing new work

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:26.900
that's not yet able to be amplified

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:31.100
because it doesn't exist yet
in more under-resourced areas.

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:32.410
I'm thinking, for example,

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:35.570
we tried to co-sponsor events and visitors

00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:37.980
and efforts through departments

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:39.520
that are already doing this work,

00:20:39.520 --> 00:20:41.430
but we've also tried
to bring units together

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:43.120
that don't talk to one another,

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:44.910
or haven't traditionally done that,

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:47.440
and we've tried to focus
attention and resources

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:49.680
on topics related to race indigeneity

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:51.530
and transnational migration

00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:54.570
like native and indigenous
studies or Latinx studies

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:57.420
that have had less pull in
established departments.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:58.990
For example, there is no department there,

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:00.580
there is a department of FM,

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:03.350
so, we tried to support FM
while also putting resources

00:21:03.350 --> 00:21:05.660
in developing areas that don't have

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:07.930
institutional support elsewhere.

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:12.930
Fourth, we've successfully
helped to bring undergraduates

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:14.920
to Yale as undergraduate students

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:16.790
and also doctoral students to Yale,

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:19.360
into small departments
over the last five years.

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:22.170
The undergraduates whom
we've helped to recruit

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:24.970
have come in part
through an awards program

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:27.410
that we've run for high school
students around the country

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:29.470
who are interested in our RITM topics

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:32.170
and doing social justice
work in their communities.

00:21:32.170 --> 00:21:34.310
And we've been part of
that recruitment process.

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:38.150
The graduate students who come
to Yale at our encouragement

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:41.510
have often come because we
offer them in our center,

00:21:41.510 --> 00:21:45.190
a home outside of the smaller department,

00:21:45.190 --> 00:21:48.340
and our home is one in
which they can fully engage

00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:49.780
topics related to race,

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:51.080
in ways that they may not be able to

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:52.260
in their home department

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:54.530
and our center is a place
where they can be assured

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:56.500
that they will see more scholars of color

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:58.840
than they likely will in
their small humanities

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:00.090
or social science department,

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:02.830
where there are often far too few of them.

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:04.980
So, that's been a method
of recruitment as well.

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:07.410
So, we've had some success in
recruitment for that reason.

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:11.630
Fifth, we've been more
successful, I think on reflection,

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:12.810
I think we've been more successful

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:15.979
at gathering graduate
students across units,

00:22:15.979 --> 00:22:18.580
than gathering faculty across units

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:20.490
who don't already know one another.

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:21.710
In fact, we've struggled

00:22:21.710 --> 00:22:25.720
with consistent faculty
participation in our center,

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:29.020
partly because faculty remains
so tied to departments.

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:30.940
Our untenured colleagues of course,

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:33.680
need to be present in
their tenuring units.

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:36.810
Our tenured colleagues already
serve on so many committees.

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:40.650
So, our efforts, unfortunately
often just invite more work

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:43.197
from our faculty colleagues.

00:22:43.197 --> 00:22:46.840
"Yes, we'd love for you
to do that conference,

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:48.450
just tell us what you wanna do."

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:49.387
Well, that's more work.

00:22:49.387 --> 00:22:51.190
"Sure, we can help with that expense,

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:53.470
please fill out this form
and send us your receipt

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:55.567
and so forth," more work.

00:22:55.567 --> 00:22:57.380
"Would you be willing to
serve on a prize committee

00:22:57.380 --> 00:22:58.213
for the center?"

00:22:58.213 --> 00:22:59.483
And so forth and so forth.

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:04.010
Sixth, and finally,
we've managed, I think,

00:23:04.010 --> 00:23:06.570
to be somewhat catalytic across campus

00:23:06.570 --> 00:23:08.360
in the spaces I've described,

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:10.590
bringing funding and what I do think of

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:13.610
as some kind of dignity
to subversive efforts

00:23:13.610 --> 00:23:15.210
in various parts of our campus,

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:17.270
or perhaps they're better
called creative efforts

00:23:17.270 --> 00:23:20.280
or efforts at greater accuracy
or something like that

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:22.040
in different departments and programs.

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:24.530
So, while we may not always
capture the attention

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:26.130
of large numbers of faculty,

00:23:26.130 --> 00:23:29.290
we have tried to be
strategic in helping faculty

00:23:29.290 --> 00:23:31.507
who wanna do interesting
and important things

00:23:31.507 --> 00:23:33.960
in ways that aren't being
supported in their units

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:35.210
to do that work.

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:37.770
In fact, we've had some
of our greatest success

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:41.820
in supporting non-ladder
track faculty on our campus

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:44.270
who are working on RITM topics.

00:23:44.270 --> 00:23:46.310
We've been able to provide
some research funding

00:23:46.310 --> 00:23:47.970
that they wouldn't get otherwise,

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:49.110
we've given them opportunities

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:51.078
to share their research with one another,

00:23:51.078 --> 00:23:53.720
and we've organized
writing groups for them

00:23:53.720 --> 00:23:55.770
that are often not available
in their home departments.

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:57.466
This may sound like small stuff,

00:23:57.466 --> 00:23:59.810
but it actually dignifies
the work of people

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:02.900
who are otherwise being
treated as contingent labor

00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:05.180
all too often on our campus and elsewhere.

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:08.170
I'd say the same with
our efforts to engage

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:09.720
with some other people on campus

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:11.210
who may not be at the top levels

00:24:11.210 --> 00:24:13.090
of their institutional hierarchies.

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:15.540
Some of our most creative
curators, for example,

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:18.150
are artists scattered
around the university

00:24:18.150 --> 00:24:20.984
who may not have the checkbook

00:24:20.984 --> 00:24:23.760
that gives them the full
funding that they want,

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:26.510
but that we can bring some of our funding

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:29.890
and some of our support to
help them realize their vision,

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:31.950
particularly visions related to reckoning,

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:34.900
which after all, is the topic
of our conversation today.

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:36.930
So, I'm gonna stop there and
pass it onto my colleagues.

00:24:36.930 --> 00:24:38.550
Thanks so much.

00:24:38.550 --> 00:24:40.020
- [Ato] Thank you so much, Stephen.

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:43.030
So, Riley, you come next.

00:24:43.030 --> 00:24:45.650
- Yes, thank you, Ato and thank
you to Jennifer and Stephen

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:47.133
for their brilliant remarks.

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:49.820
It's such a pleasure

00:24:49.820 --> 00:24:52.060
to be in conversation with my colleagues,

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:53.640
the center in race consortium,

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:56.260
and it's my privilege to work
with the incredible staff

00:24:56.260 --> 00:24:58.710
at the Center For The Study
of Race, Politics and Culture,

00:24:58.710 --> 00:25:01.010
Tracy Matthews, Maryland
Willis, Jacquelyn Gaines,

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:02.870
and Tiara Kilpatrick.

00:25:02.870 --> 00:25:06.690
As an opening statement, I wanna
provide some of the context

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:10.140
and a sense of the day-to-day
of the center's activities

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:12.740
as a way to ground some of
the intellectual, political

00:25:12.740 --> 00:25:15.190
and ethical work as I see it.

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:17.560
I also wanna acknowledge that
the University of Chicago

00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:18.990
sits on the tribal homelands

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:20.600
of the Council of the Three Fires,

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:24.770
the Odawa, Ojibwe and Potawatomi nations,

00:25:24.770 --> 00:25:26.290
as well as other tribes.

00:25:26.290 --> 00:25:29.990
And as the sixth largest urban
native community in the US,

00:25:29.990 --> 00:25:32.410
indigenous people continue
to practice their heritage,

00:25:32.410 --> 00:25:35.483
traditions and care for
the land and waterways.

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:38.460
The Center For The Study of
Race, Politics and Culture,

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:43.460
CSRPC, was established by my
colleague, Michael C. Dawson,

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:47.030
professor of political science in 1996.

00:25:47.030 --> 00:25:50.160
And from its inception,
CSRPC has been committed

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:54.400
to establishing and sustaining
a space to center and expand

00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:56.230
the study of race and ethnicity

00:25:56.230 --> 00:25:57.930
at the University of Chicago,

00:25:57.930 --> 00:26:00.560
by fostering interdisciplinary,
transnational,

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:04.530
and intersectional research,
teaching and public debate.

00:26:04.530 --> 00:26:06.570
So, at the center, we understand
that race and ethnicity

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:09.300
affect every idea and interaction

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:11.580
and we work to foster meaningful dialogues

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:13.210
about the myriad ways race and ethnicity

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:17.010
inform our lived
experiences and scholarship.

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:18.650
The center is committed
to providing funding

00:26:18.650 --> 00:26:19.770
and other forms of support

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:23.580
like many of my colleagues
working at other centers

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:25.940
for projects that are initiated
by faculty affiliates,

00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:28.490
graduate students, undergraduates,
artists, and residents,

00:26:28.490 --> 00:26:29.770
and visiting fellows.

00:26:29.770 --> 00:26:32.080
And I'm especially proud that this year,

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:35.310
in addition to our ongoing
artists and residents' program,

00:26:35.310 --> 00:26:37.140
which we've been doing in
collaboration with arts

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:38.250
and public life,

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:41.610
that we welcomed our
inaugural practitioner fellows

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:44.960
in conjunction with the
posing human rights lab.

00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:46.830
As part of their fellowship,

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:49.130
each practitioner is designing a project

00:26:49.130 --> 00:26:52.163
that explores an aspect
of the carceral system.

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:54.590
By doing this kind of work,

00:26:54.590 --> 00:26:57.410
we continue to strengthen
community relations

00:26:57.410 --> 00:26:59.960
with the south side and Chicago

00:26:59.960 --> 00:27:03.410
as we continue to act in
solidarity with local,

00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:06.200
national and global struggles for racial,

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:09.563
ethnic, economic, gender
sexual, and social justice.

00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:12.070
So, in addition to providing funding

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:13.770
and other forms of support for scholarship

00:27:13.770 --> 00:27:15.650
overseeing an undergraduate
major and minor,

00:27:15.650 --> 00:27:17.730
hosting residencies and visiting fellows,

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:20.660
sponsoring workshops and working groups,

00:27:20.660 --> 00:27:22.730
which include the reproduction of race

00:27:22.730 --> 00:27:25.410
and racial ideology workshop,
the race working group,

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:28.180
which is a graduate led
and graduate-only space,

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:30.000
the mass incarceration working group

00:27:31.070 --> 00:27:33.450
and the race and pedagogy working group

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:36.270
and the slavery and visual
culture working group,

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:39.130
the center supports and sponsors
a number of public programs

00:27:39.130 --> 00:27:42.240
geared toward promoting an
investigation of the ties

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:44.530
between race, ethnicity, and culture,

00:27:44.530 --> 00:27:46.490
often in collaboration
with student groups,

00:27:46.490 --> 00:27:48.680
other campus centers and academic units,

00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:51.490
as well as community-based institutions.

00:27:51.490 --> 00:27:54.370
This is the day-to-day work of the center.

00:27:54.370 --> 00:27:59.120
And yet we're also ethically
and morally compelled

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:01.660
to be responsive to our
current environments,

00:28:01.660 --> 00:28:04.450
to provide forums for
activists and scholars,

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:08.230
to give context and share
strategies for combating racist,

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:12.403
sexist, homophobic, and
transphobic injustice and violence.

00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:14.850
Unlike some of the other schools

00:28:14.850 --> 00:28:16.660
in the centering race consortium,

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:19.410
the University of Chicago
does not have a department

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:21.700
devoted to the study
of race and ethnicity.

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:24.330
However, through the committed work

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:27.000
of some of CSRP's faculty affiliates

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:29.650
working within the more
than diversity campaign

00:28:29.650 --> 00:28:31.890
and in solidarity with years of organizing

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:35.210
from U Chicago students
with U Chicago United

00:28:35.210 --> 00:28:38.800
and particularly ethnic studies
now, the grad student union,

00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:40.950
and the black grad student collective,

00:28:40.950 --> 00:28:43.450
there has been some
progress made on that front,

00:28:43.450 --> 00:28:46.610
which allows for us at the
center to begin to engage

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:50.460
in the related process of
imagining a renewed center

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:53.000
that works alongside
an emergent department.

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:55.100
And I'd like to pause here,

00:28:55.100 --> 00:28:57.730
but will be happy to return
to some of these issues later,

00:28:57.730 --> 00:29:01.120
as I think this moment at
the University of Chicago

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:05.090
allows us to think about
reckoning on a number of registers

00:29:05.090 --> 00:29:09.860
and perhaps also to consider
what comes after a reckoning.

00:29:09.860 --> 00:29:10.693
Thank you.

00:29:13.260 --> 00:29:15.477
- [Ato] Thank you so much, Riley.

00:29:15.477 --> 00:29:18.310
So, Tricia, you're next.

00:29:18.310 --> 00:29:19.760
- Thank you, Ato.

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:22.900
Thank you, my dear colleagues,
members of the CRC.

00:29:22.900 --> 00:29:24.730
It's been great working with you

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:28.220
and fantastic hearing you
here and planning this event.

00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:31.150
Thanks for everyone
who's come out on this.

00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:34.653
At least in my part of
the country, rainy day.

00:29:35.736 --> 00:29:38.100
I'm gonna try to focus my comments

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:42.560
on a broader set of questions and problems

00:29:42.560 --> 00:29:46.150
that don't entirely always
dovetail with my own center,

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:49.163
but when they do, I'll
certainly emphasize it.

00:29:50.064 --> 00:29:52.460
And that has to do with what I would call

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:54.680
the consequences of the legacy

00:29:54.680 --> 00:29:57.343
of the formation of race centers.

00:30:02.470 --> 00:30:04.830
All of the centers represented here

00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:07.780
have tremendous faculty support

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:10.723
and amazing work comes
out of these places,

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:13.880
graduate student investments,
so on and so forth.

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:16.260
So, I'm not in any way disparaging

00:30:16.260 --> 00:30:18.110
that level of intellectual investment.

00:30:18.110 --> 00:30:21.780
But what concerns me is the
degree to which that work

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:24.180
is undermined by the very structures

00:30:24.180 --> 00:30:27.320
that force us into the
placement that we're in.

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:29.320
So, I wanna talk a bit about that first.

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:33.990
The formation of the centers

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:37.280
that study race and ethnicity
broadly in the United States

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:40.900
almost always emerge
from a student protest

00:30:40.900 --> 00:30:42.540
first and foremost.

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:46.140
And everyone here had mentioned
that at one form or another,

00:30:46.140 --> 00:30:47.980
either in the past or in the present.

00:30:47.980 --> 00:30:51.180
I was like, no, it's an ongoing project,

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:52.360
but I wanna say a little bit more

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:54.810
about what that student activism is about

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:58.100
because it often becomes a touchstone,

00:30:58.100 --> 00:31:01.910
but it's a very important
part of the critique

00:31:01.910 --> 00:31:04.383
of disciplinary knowledge
in the university.

00:31:05.729 --> 00:31:08.830
Eventually, students of
color of various types

00:31:08.830 --> 00:31:12.210
come to the university
only in the last 50 years

00:31:12.210 --> 00:31:15.670
and they realize that the
structures of knowledge

00:31:15.670 --> 00:31:17.980
in fact, are not in any
way designed to shed light

00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:20.810
on the histories of the
groups out of which they come.

00:31:20.810 --> 00:31:25.810
And that kind of protest is
usually focused specifically

00:31:26.140 --> 00:31:28.130
on demanding that the curriculum

00:31:28.130 --> 00:31:32.520
provide significant
educational sources for them.

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:35.520
And in addition,

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:38.100
it's a challenge to the
role of universities,

00:31:38.100 --> 00:31:40.640
the social protests channels
to the role of universities

00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:42.910
in the given town, in a given city

00:31:42.910 --> 00:31:47.730
and its role in being a
sort of not in alignment

00:31:47.730 --> 00:31:49.990
with social justice
movements in the street

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:51.253
and in the public sphere.

00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:55.130
And so, it's an implicit critique

00:31:55.130 --> 00:31:57.530
about the role of race in the discipline.

00:31:57.530 --> 00:31:58.740
And that's why this notion

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:01.260
of institutionalizing
critical race studies

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:04.020
is such an important part of the framing.

00:32:04.020 --> 00:32:05.890
It's not simply to study race

00:32:05.890 --> 00:32:09.040
because that is actually going
on every once in a while,

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:11.340
but in ways that was actually reproducing

00:32:11.340 --> 00:32:13.210
the very problems that
we're talking about.

00:32:13.210 --> 00:32:15.673
Jennifer talked a bit
about this at the outset,

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:20.243
in terms of anthropology
and so on and object.

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:26.200
So, I just wanna really put
the work that we're doing

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:31.640
in the context of disciplines
that are structurally hostile,

00:32:32.252 --> 00:32:35.480
even in the cases when you have,

00:32:35.480 --> 00:32:37.860
well-meaning faculty members
in those departments,

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:41.680
I'm not making an against
every academic in a discipline

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:43.830
of United States, but I'm more saying

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:45.170
that I think it's really important

00:32:45.170 --> 00:32:48.733
to talk about the institutional
tension that's there.

00:32:51.610 --> 00:32:56.230
The space of centered on race,

00:32:56.230 --> 00:32:58.640
it seems to me are really developed

00:32:58.640 --> 00:33:02.810
in the inters disease of
the university as a whole.

00:33:02.810 --> 00:33:06.120
That there's a very little
space that is cracked open,

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:10.570
and we endeavor to inhabit
it, to give it life,

00:33:10.570 --> 00:33:12.720
to figure out what's needed

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:15.650
in our particular local circumstances.

00:33:15.650 --> 00:33:19.940
But that process does
result in a number of things

00:33:19.940 --> 00:33:22.400
that I think are exceptionally important.

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:26.090
One is that it's built in fragility

00:33:26.090 --> 00:33:30.360
and that fragility is registered
in lots of different ways.

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:33.350
It's registered in terms
of funding and support,

00:33:33.350 --> 00:33:36.120
space, whether or not centers

00:33:36.120 --> 00:33:40.370
or interdisciplinary spaces can be homes

00:33:40.370 --> 00:33:43.563
for tenure track positions
in all or in part,

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:46.980
it's about whether or not it can be seen

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:50.690
as a valuable and important
part of the university

00:33:50.690 --> 00:33:52.910
and its forms of knowledge production,

00:33:52.910 --> 00:33:54.450
and that matters a lot for whether or not

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:57.990
faculty from other departments
can be involved safely

00:33:57.990 --> 00:34:02.150
and professionally, it's understaffed,

00:34:02.150 --> 00:34:04.857
and then it's often frankly, trotted out

00:34:04.857 --> 00:34:07.880
and this is over and over again
across 50 years trotted out

00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:12.140
at the prices of the decade
or the prices of the year

00:34:12.140 --> 00:34:15.763
to hold and be the center of discussion.

00:34:16.600 --> 00:34:18.520
Now, I will admit at Brown,

00:34:18.520 --> 00:34:21.950
I've had tremendous support for
the center during my tenure,

00:34:21.950 --> 00:34:26.540
but CSREA has been around
since the mid 1980s, 1986,

00:34:26.540 --> 00:34:30.700
and for a lot of that time
had extremely limited support

00:34:30.700 --> 00:34:34.640
and was in many ways starved
off the necessary resources

00:34:34.640 --> 00:34:37.190
to do a number of the
things it wanted to do.

00:34:37.190 --> 00:34:40.900
It however was a very successful incubator

00:34:40.900 --> 00:34:43.710
for the ethnic studies
concentration at Brown.

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:46.300
And so that's been very important advance

00:34:46.300 --> 00:34:47.870
that happened before,

00:34:47.870 --> 00:34:52.870
but the level of work that it
takes to keep this crack open

00:34:54.890 --> 00:34:57.360
for all of us is very significant.

00:34:57.360 --> 00:34:59.910
And it not only requires the immense work

00:34:59.910 --> 00:35:02.510
of our colleagues who donate their time

00:35:02.510 --> 00:35:05.520
as Steve was pointing
out to various endeavors,

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:08.060
but it means that those
of us who direct them

00:35:08.060 --> 00:35:13.060
aren't simply directing in
a climate of presumed value

00:35:14.110 --> 00:35:15.780
and dignity and significance,

00:35:15.780 --> 00:35:19.400
for one has to perpetually
explain the value

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:21.610
and significance and why,

00:35:21.610 --> 00:35:23.940
even in cases when centers for race

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:27.170
are placed in campaigns for endowments,

00:35:27.170 --> 00:35:28.530
which is extremely rare,

00:35:28.530 --> 00:35:31.160
but even in the cases when that happens,

00:35:31.160 --> 00:35:33.470
one has to explain over and over again,

00:35:33.470 --> 00:35:37.840
what one does and why it's
a central relevant issue

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:42.405
and why would any white people
wanna give money to this,

00:35:42.405 --> 00:35:43.940
for example, which if I could tell you

00:35:43.940 --> 00:35:46.060
how many times I've had that conversation.

00:35:46.060 --> 00:35:48.590
So, for me, this means that the reckoning

00:35:48.590 --> 00:35:51.650
is not so much about more centered

00:35:51.650 --> 00:35:55.970
and more of the repetition of
the structures that we're in,

00:35:55.970 --> 00:36:00.390
it's really about a fundamental challenge

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:03.940
to the construction of race
and the marginality of race,

00:36:03.940 --> 00:36:06.300
to the thinking and the framing

00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:09.353
of the vast majority of traditional
disciplinary knowledges.

00:36:10.190 --> 00:36:13.620
And that can't just be adding
on a section in the curriculum

00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:15.220
or adding on one course,

00:36:15.220 --> 00:36:18.820
it has to be a very significant reframing

00:36:18.820 --> 00:36:21.930
of what these disciplines are
about, perhaps a dismantling,

00:36:21.930 --> 00:36:24.140
which is what my colleague, Matt Guterl

00:36:24.140 --> 00:36:25.793
was talking about yesterday.

00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:31.347
But I think this
reckoning can't just be at

00:36:31.347 --> 00:36:33.810
hoping students are gonna protest enough

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:35.890
to expand more attention

00:36:35.890 --> 00:36:40.890
or that we'll be able to have
a friendly administration

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:44.640
which would be lovely and in my case is,

00:36:44.640 --> 00:36:49.550
but what I think the reckoning
really has to be about

00:36:49.550 --> 00:36:52.260
is is a real fundamental infrastructural

00:36:52.260 --> 00:36:55.457
intellectual challenge to the
whole enterprise to say that

00:36:55.457 --> 00:36:58.410
"If you think you can do discipline X

00:36:58.410 --> 00:37:01.320
and not fundamentally
think about race, gender,

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:04.100
and sexuality, indigeneity, and so on

00:37:04.100 --> 00:37:07.380
as significant parts
of how your discipline

00:37:07.380 --> 00:37:09.810
was either shaped or how
it's functioning now,

00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:11.750
then you're not doing
the work you need to do."

00:37:11.750 --> 00:37:15.540
It has to be that level of
racial reckoning, in my opinion,

00:37:15.540 --> 00:37:19.730
otherwise, we will be in
very much the same position,

00:37:19.730 --> 00:37:24.040
25 years from now with a next
generation of brilliant people

00:37:24.040 --> 00:37:26.130
who will replace my
brilliant colleagues here

00:37:26.130 --> 00:37:30.510
and for sure, me, and
they will say very much

00:37:30.510 --> 00:37:34.200
the same thing, maybe with
different academic language,

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:35.980
but they'll be in a very similar position.

00:37:35.980 --> 00:37:40.040
And that ultimately is
a stabilizing phenomena.

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:42.500
So, that our fragility, our underfunding,

00:37:42.500 --> 00:37:46.350
and yet our constant repetition
of protest and inclusion

00:37:46.350 --> 00:37:49.440
and so on and so, it's
a dance of repetition

00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:52.990
that I think doesn't change the outcomes

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:55.250
in significant ways.

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:59.940
And so I wanna end with a real challenge

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:04.566
that we don't necessarily
wanna blow up our centers,

00:38:04.566 --> 00:38:08.900
but there's a sense in which our demise

00:38:08.900 --> 00:38:11.320
has to be part of our plan

00:38:11.320 --> 00:38:14.890
in that we have to
imagine that departments

00:38:14.890 --> 00:38:17.160
can be so fundamentally changed,

00:38:17.160 --> 00:38:19.470
that we would be part of that change

00:38:19.470 --> 00:38:24.350
rather than keeping this tension
between margin and center

00:38:24.350 --> 00:38:25.360
in terms of the disciplines.

00:38:25.360 --> 00:38:26.410
So, I'm gonna stop there

00:38:26.410 --> 00:38:29.020
'cause I know we're talking a while,

00:38:29.020 --> 00:38:31.958
but I'm looking forward to
our conversation as a whole.

00:38:31.958 --> 00:38:32.791
Thank you very much.

00:38:32.791 --> 00:38:36.880
- Thank, you so much, Tricia,
and to all the panelists.

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:39.470
This has been magnificent.

00:38:39.470 --> 00:38:43.760
Now I have a set of questions
to ask to open up debates

00:38:43.760 --> 00:38:45.360
and I have the questions here,

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:49.530
but I'm going to pose one
of my own that I fabricated

00:38:49.530 --> 00:38:52.200
as I was listening to you guys.

00:38:52.200 --> 00:38:56.150
And it has to do with,
to put it very simply,

00:38:56.150 --> 00:38:59.820
the problem of proposition making

00:38:59.820 --> 00:39:04.330
in interdisciplinary studies
because all these centers

00:39:04.330 --> 00:39:07.010
are at heart, interdisciplinary.

00:39:07.010 --> 00:39:10.000
Now, what do I mean by the
problem of proposition making?

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:12.110
I am in an English department.

00:39:12.110 --> 00:39:13.540
Now in an English department,

00:39:13.540 --> 00:39:16.390
and I'm sure that Riley will see echoes

00:39:16.390 --> 00:39:19.720
of his own department English at Chicago,

00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:22.020
in what I'm going to say,

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:24.640
Shakespearean and what
I'm going to say now

00:39:25.480 --> 00:39:27.810
align specifically with Shakespeare

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:29.370
and Medieval studies.

00:39:29.370 --> 00:39:34.370
A Shakespearean to make a
proposition on Shakespeare

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:38.680
for it to be salient in the
field of Shakespeare studies

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:41.163
will have to master several languages,

00:39:42.680 --> 00:39:45.080
Latin and Greek among them,

00:39:45.080 --> 00:39:48.800
will also have to master
different discourses,

00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:53.740
medical history,
sometimes music and so on,

00:39:53.740 --> 00:39:58.740
but one of this signature
features of Shakespeareans

00:39:58.866 --> 00:40:00.303
is that they're etymologists.

00:40:00.303 --> 00:40:04.670
They like the diving
into the meaning of words

00:40:04.670 --> 00:40:05.590
in Shakespeare.

00:40:05.590 --> 00:40:07.680
There's no serious Shakespearean

00:40:07.680 --> 00:40:09.980
who doesn't know
something about etymology,

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:12.350
for a very simple and practical reason.

00:40:12.350 --> 00:40:16.010
The language of Shakespeare
is 450 years old.

00:40:16.010 --> 00:40:18.670
Now, if you shift from the medieval,

00:40:18.670 --> 00:40:20.760
and the medievalists are also similar.

00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:25.760
If you shift from medieval
studies and Shakespeare studies

00:40:26.300 --> 00:40:28.850
to say, post colonialism my own area,

00:40:28.850 --> 00:40:31.800
our protocols of proposition
making a different,

00:40:31.800 --> 00:40:34.740
they are more advocacy oriented.

00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:39.740
They're more shining a
torchlight on inequality

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:43.410
and colonialism and so on and so forth.

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:47.270
Our protocols of proposition
making are so different

00:40:47.270 --> 00:40:50.630
that it is not unusual
in English departments

00:40:50.630 --> 00:40:53.750
for shakespeareans and
medievalists to think

00:40:53.750 --> 00:40:57.160
that they're in completely
different department

00:40:57.160 --> 00:40:59.740
than concerns from say, Shakespeareans.

00:40:59.740 --> 00:41:01.293
My question is then this,

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:05.190
so this is back to what Tricia said

00:41:05.190 --> 00:41:09.830
about we should contemplate
the demise of our,

00:41:09.830 --> 00:41:13.130
or the non-existence
of our various centers

00:41:13.130 --> 00:41:14.900
of ethnic studies,

00:41:14.900 --> 00:41:19.410
what would it take for the
protocols of proposition making

00:41:19.410 --> 00:41:24.410
in anthropology, in sociology, in English,

00:41:25.010 --> 00:41:28.780
in the various disciplines to admit race

00:41:29.837 --> 00:41:32.927
as an aspect of proposition making?

00:41:32.927 --> 00:41:36.233
And this is not simply
about looking at race

00:41:36.233 --> 00:41:41.233
because you could look at
race and still not change

00:41:41.450 --> 00:41:43.930
the way that you make your propositions.

00:41:43.930 --> 00:41:47.850
So, this is a question about
intellectual paradigms,

00:41:50.720 --> 00:41:53.660
it's not merely additive, it's not merely,

00:41:53.660 --> 00:41:55.720
let us talk about race now,

00:41:55.720 --> 00:41:57.960
but it's about the more,

00:41:57.960 --> 00:42:00.200
so for example, the postcolonial,

00:42:00.200 --> 00:42:01.910
I talk about race completely differently

00:42:01.910 --> 00:42:04.123
from how a Shakesperean

00:42:04.123 --> 00:42:07.300
or even a classicist talks about race

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:09.310
because it's completely different

00:42:09.310 --> 00:42:12.280
because the genealogies
of the idea of race

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:14.840
in the classics are different.

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:16.650
What would you,

00:42:16.650 --> 00:42:21.570
so this is my invitation for
you to lend your thoughts,

00:42:21.570 --> 00:42:25.370
to the problematic of proposition making

00:42:25.370 --> 00:42:30.370
in our interdisciplinary race
and equity, CCSRE, and so on,

00:42:32.790 --> 00:42:37.340
but in relation to how our
protocols of proposition making

00:42:37.340 --> 00:42:40.770
might make sense within
the individual disciplines.

00:42:40.770 --> 00:42:42.773
So, this is my question to start with.

00:42:43.820 --> 00:42:48.820
We can start from the
same order of speaking.

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:50.720
So, Jennifer can respond.

00:42:52.381 --> 00:42:55.210
- Just briefly and I do think
it's a related question,

00:42:55.210 --> 00:42:57.640
my father was a social
scientist and he used to say

00:42:57.640 --> 00:43:00.590
that my questions began where his ended

00:43:00.590 --> 00:43:04.243
in terms of being a quantitative person.

00:43:04.243 --> 00:43:09.243
And my greatest, I think
discussion that we would have

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:14.230
precisely this question was
when he was writing along

00:43:14.230 --> 00:43:18.520
chapter on Gordon's
theory of G in psychology

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:21.580
and had the good fortune
to put in at least,

00:43:21.580 --> 00:43:25.560
and of course this was taken
up and this was a racist idea.

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:29.530
Gordon was a eugenicist
and he said that had he not

00:43:29.530 --> 00:43:31.530
been in discussions with me over time,

00:43:31.530 --> 00:43:34.730
that that would have been
a "irrelevant detail"

00:43:34.730 --> 00:43:39.730
to explaining how that term worked.

00:43:39.790 --> 00:43:41.920
And similarly, even with
Shakespeare studies,

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:43.940
I know, the work of Kim Hall,

00:43:43.940 --> 00:43:45.790
we were talking about this yesterday,

00:43:46.930 --> 00:43:49.010
even in terms of etymology,

00:43:49.010 --> 00:43:52.450
Peter Stolley Ross was actually
my dissertation advisor

00:43:52.450 --> 00:43:56.680
who was a Shakespearian, very
much interested in entomology,

00:43:56.680 --> 00:44:00.620
but where that takes you is
not just a classical myth,

00:44:00.620 --> 00:44:04.507
but to very key terms such
as what is a blackamoor.

00:44:05.460 --> 00:44:07.840
And so we are always, I think,

00:44:07.840 --> 00:44:10.890
already engaged even the very notion

00:44:10.890 --> 00:44:15.330
of an English department with England

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:16.750
and the English language.

00:44:16.750 --> 00:44:18.970
If you really get into
it in medieval studies,

00:44:18.970 --> 00:44:22.330
this came up yesterday,
Geraldine Hangsworker,

00:44:22.330 --> 00:44:25.030
that really opens up
again to a global world

00:44:25.030 --> 00:44:28.360
in which race is produced discursively.

00:44:28.360 --> 00:44:29.980
And I think it was Noemie that yesterday

00:44:29.980 --> 00:44:34.140
was talking about the fact
that who is asking the question

00:44:35.110 --> 00:44:38.720
can sometimes change the very notion

00:44:38.720 --> 00:44:41.570
of what is important to know,

00:44:41.570 --> 00:44:43.150
which is what I was trying to say.

00:44:43.150 --> 00:44:45.250
And I think that it speaks also very much

00:44:45.250 --> 00:44:48.210
to everything everyone has said here.

00:44:48.210 --> 00:44:50.850
And Tricia saying that what would happen

00:44:50.850 --> 00:44:55.850
if we completely changed what
we think a university is for

00:44:55.970 --> 00:44:58.470
whom does it serve, what is this about,

00:44:58.470 --> 00:45:00.560
why are we engaged in this kind of work?

00:45:00.560 --> 00:45:02.480
And we've seen this with the increase

00:45:02.480 --> 00:45:04.080
of the global university.

00:45:04.080 --> 00:45:06.320
And I think that that's
why that term reckoning,

00:45:06.320 --> 00:45:10.810
which is also talking about
the distribution of funds

00:45:10.810 --> 00:45:14.943
and sources and value is so important.

00:45:16.656 --> 00:45:18.100
Thank you for that question.

00:45:18.100 --> 00:45:20.033
- [Ato] Thank you, Jennifer, Steven?

00:45:21.520 --> 00:45:23.470
- This is a great question, thank you.

00:45:23.470 --> 00:45:26.120
There's so much to say on this.

00:45:26.120 --> 00:45:28.850
I think I'll just try to
keep it relatively simple

00:45:28.850 --> 00:45:30.840
and say that I think it is,

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:34.100
to me, I always come back to
words like, dignity and value,

00:45:34.100 --> 00:45:38.340
which Jennifer and Tricia,
I think Riley and everybody

00:45:38.340 --> 00:45:40.210
uses very commonly.

00:45:40.210 --> 00:45:45.210
I think to me, it's about
enfranchising points of view,

00:45:45.930 --> 00:45:47.570
dignifying those points of view,

00:45:47.570 --> 00:45:52.570
allowing formations and
understandings to take center stage

00:45:53.550 --> 00:45:57.550
in university-wide and
societal conversations,

00:45:57.550 --> 00:46:01.300
but actually minoritize or
diminish the prominence of

00:46:03.760 --> 00:46:07.580
historically rooted, but
narrow ways of thinking

00:46:07.580 --> 00:46:11.260
about problems that are
very much departmentalized.

00:46:11.260 --> 00:46:14.670
I'm fortunate at Yale that I
actually work in a university

00:46:14.670 --> 00:46:16.730
that we have certainly
have a lot of problems

00:46:16.730 --> 00:46:20.560
around interdisciplinarity and
recognize interdisciplinary,

00:46:20.560 --> 00:46:23.520
but we have some very wonderful
interdisciplinary programs

00:46:23.520 --> 00:46:25.490
and they have prominence.

00:46:25.490 --> 00:46:28.670
And I often say that
many, I'm a historian,

00:46:28.670 --> 00:46:30.420
and one of my appointments is in history,

00:46:30.420 --> 00:46:32.470
but I often say that many
of our best historians

00:46:32.470 --> 00:46:34.930
on our campus are not in
the history department.

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:37.710
And I'd like to see
our students in history

00:46:37.710 --> 00:46:39.930
actually work with them as well,

00:46:39.930 --> 00:46:42.020
even as they're getting their degree

00:46:42.020 --> 00:46:43.470
and their credential in the department

00:46:43.470 --> 00:46:45.000
to which they were admitted,

00:46:45.000 --> 00:46:47.190
I'm thinking mostly of
graduate students here.

00:46:47.190 --> 00:46:48.700
So, I think that centers

00:46:50.870 --> 00:46:52.670
can work in a lot of different ways.

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:56.653
I'm all for the revolution
and hopefully working for it.

00:46:57.500 --> 00:46:59.030
And I think that the idea

00:46:59.030 --> 00:47:03.140
of the withering away of departments

00:47:04.090 --> 00:47:07.690
is absolutely something to be
anticipated and pushed toward.

00:47:07.690 --> 00:47:10.430
I also think that we make
small gains in departments

00:47:10.430 --> 00:47:12.430
by dignifying people's work,

00:47:12.430 --> 00:47:15.840
who actually very much
identify with their discipline

00:47:15.840 --> 00:47:17.970
and wanna see their future in a discipline

00:47:17.970 --> 00:47:21.890
in ways that I may not be the thing

00:47:21.890 --> 00:47:23.710
I think of as most productive.

00:47:23.710 --> 00:47:27.810
And yet we're talking about
the politics of small numbers.

00:47:27.810 --> 00:47:29.840
There's relatively few of us.

00:47:29.840 --> 00:47:32.178
And so I wanna see our center, for example

00:47:32.178 --> 00:47:35.410
working with and helping
move forward conversations

00:47:35.410 --> 00:47:37.720
that may be very disciplinarily bound

00:47:37.720 --> 00:47:40.820
and that I think may
not be the longterm ways

00:47:40.820 --> 00:47:41.963
in which we wanna go,

00:47:41.963 --> 00:47:43.510
but that actually, I think do bring value

00:47:43.510 --> 00:47:47.090
because I want that particular
faculty member to prosper

00:47:47.090 --> 00:47:49.150
in their local department.

00:47:49.150 --> 00:47:51.000
And I know that if they're prospering,

00:47:51.000 --> 00:47:53.380
they're bringing a couple
of students along with them.

00:47:53.380 --> 00:47:54.890
So, I think we can do that work,

00:47:54.890 --> 00:47:59.310
which may be additive
and not revolutionary

00:47:59.310 --> 00:48:01.270
alongside doing the revolutionary work,

00:48:01.270 --> 00:48:04.810
which is more about privileging
other ways of knowing,

00:48:04.810 --> 00:48:07.730
other methodologies, other connections

00:48:07.730 --> 00:48:12.730
that are different problems of proposition

00:48:12.760 --> 00:48:14.197
as you were putting them.

00:48:14.197 --> 00:48:17.960
And I think it's the
challenge is to be open

00:48:17.960 --> 00:48:20.070
to doing all of those
kinds of work at once.

00:48:20.070 --> 00:48:23.910
And so, anyway, that's where
we are, I think, on our campus.

00:48:23.910 --> 00:48:27.840
And it leaves us sometimes
unclear about next steps

00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:31.090
and priorities, because
there is so much work to do,

00:48:31.090 --> 00:48:35.200
but we don't wanna
foreclose what may seem like

00:48:35.200 --> 00:48:37.733
the more cautious or more
conservative approach,

00:48:39.770 --> 00:48:43.060
because it's not radical
or revolutionary enough.

00:48:43.060 --> 00:48:46.100
We wanna think about
ways to do all of that.

00:48:46.100 --> 00:48:46.980
- [Ato] Thank you, Stephen.

00:48:46.980 --> 00:48:49.430
Actually, there's a line that you used,

00:48:49.430 --> 00:48:53.070
which I used to think a little bit more

00:48:53.070 --> 00:48:56.103
is enfranchising different points of view.

00:48:57.710 --> 00:48:59.950
You said this in your response,

00:48:59.950 --> 00:49:02.840
enfranchising different points of view,

00:49:02.840 --> 00:49:07.103
a whole lot of weight can
be put on the question

00:49:09.510 --> 00:49:12.210
of how to enfranchise
different points of view

00:49:13.176 --> 00:49:16.060
as an aspect of proposition
making in the discipline,

00:49:16.060 --> 00:49:19.820
because it's true, in
English literary studies,

00:49:19.820 --> 00:49:22.600
they have historically not been very open

00:49:22.600 --> 00:49:26.200
to enfranchising minority points of view

00:49:26.200 --> 00:49:29.190
of which they are mainly
even in the dominant specs.

00:49:29.190 --> 00:49:30.023
So, the question

00:49:30.023 --> 00:49:32.750
of how to enfranchise
different points of view

00:49:32.750 --> 00:49:34.541
is really important.

00:49:34.541 --> 00:49:36.020
Riley, please.

00:49:36.020 --> 00:49:41.020
- Thanks and I was also really
struck by that formulation.

00:49:41.920 --> 00:49:44.960
In some ways, it also
underscores how I think

00:49:44.960 --> 00:49:47.780
about what it means to
do work at the center,

00:49:47.780 --> 00:49:51.850
which is that we are a space
for Maximalist approaches

00:49:51.850 --> 00:49:53.090
to race and ethnicity,

00:49:53.090 --> 00:49:55.890
that there are many
ways through many modes

00:49:55.890 --> 00:49:58.853
that people are engaging in
these questions critically.

00:49:59.860 --> 00:50:04.860
To that and in concert,
I hope with the comments

00:50:05.240 --> 00:50:08.890
that Jennifer and Steve have already made,

00:50:08.890 --> 00:50:11.560
I'll just add that some
part of your question

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:13.130
is also making me think a little bit

00:50:13.130 --> 00:50:15.177
about some of the fields that I work in,

00:50:15.177 --> 00:50:19.053
for which the matter of
institutionalization is not settled.

00:50:20.300 --> 00:50:23.110
To be working in the
field of trans studies

00:50:23.110 --> 00:50:27.800
is not really seen as
having a particular way

00:50:27.800 --> 00:50:30.090
in which a proposition might be made.

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:34.510
And so I'm also deeply interested in,

00:50:34.510 --> 00:50:36.420
or have been hearing echoed

00:50:36.420 --> 00:50:39.070
throughout the course of the last two days

00:50:39.070 --> 00:50:41.110
the provocation from Stewart Hall

00:50:41.110 --> 00:50:46.010
about what it is, for him,
what cultural studies would do

00:50:46.010 --> 00:50:48.530
for example, to a
discipline like sociology,

00:50:48.530 --> 00:50:52.300
which is in some ways it
was to cannibalize it,

00:50:52.300 --> 00:50:54.800
but in so doing to make it anew.

00:50:54.800 --> 00:50:56.940
And I think often,

00:50:56.940 --> 00:50:59.893
sitting and being trained
in inter disciplines,

00:51:00.850 --> 00:51:02.663
the work then becomes,

00:51:05.210 --> 00:51:08.380
it's obviously always
still rife with power,

00:51:08.380 --> 00:51:11.270
but it's also about a
project about legibility

00:51:11.270 --> 00:51:12.280
and audiences.

00:51:12.280 --> 00:51:14.380
I think as Jennifer mentioned as well,

00:51:14.380 --> 00:51:18.740
who are you talking to as
another note to Noemie,

00:51:18.740 --> 00:51:21.550
but I really appreciate the
question as an invitation

00:51:21.550 --> 00:51:24.403
to think along these ideas.

00:51:25.310 --> 00:51:26.810
- [Ato] Thanks, Riley, Tricia?

00:51:30.160 --> 00:51:32.150
- I guess I just have two additions

00:51:32.150 --> 00:51:34.203
to these wonderful remarks.

00:51:35.640 --> 00:51:39.090
The first is that disciplinary boundaries

00:51:39.090 --> 00:51:41.600
are somewhat arbitrary.

00:51:41.600 --> 00:51:43.350
They have historical formations,

00:51:43.350 --> 00:51:47.320
but sometimes you have different
propositional formations

00:51:47.320 --> 00:51:48.800
in the same discipline,

00:51:48.800 --> 00:51:52.460
and they're not necessarily
limited because of it.

00:51:52.460 --> 00:51:54.680
What that means is that disciplines

00:51:54.680 --> 00:51:57.989
are political skirmishes.

00:51:57.989 --> 00:52:00.890
Really they're the result of
winning political skirmishes

00:52:01.940 --> 00:52:04.930
and as such, they shouldn't
be treated as gospel

00:52:04.930 --> 00:52:08.990
and they should be understood
as being transformational

00:52:08.990 --> 00:52:11.500
or formation of one form or another.

00:52:11.500 --> 00:52:16.200
So when you say can the
protocols and proposition making,

00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:20.083
that might come out of a
critical race studies framework,

00:52:20.940 --> 00:52:22.502
where can they be?

00:52:22.502 --> 00:52:25.502
Well, they can be in a lot of
places for the reasons I said.

00:52:26.920 --> 00:52:28.880
The second thing is that,

00:52:28.880 --> 00:52:33.400
I totally wanna support
this initial point,

00:52:33.400 --> 00:52:34.707
which was, look, you do both.

00:52:34.707 --> 00:52:38.373
And you do all you have
to do at this moment.

00:52:39.460 --> 00:52:42.100
And we've been very fortunate

00:52:42.100 --> 00:52:44.910
to recruit some amazing
scholars on race to Brown

00:52:44.910 --> 00:52:49.910
in the last five to eight years
who've increased the ranks

00:52:49.970 --> 00:52:52.993
of what was already a really
amazing group of people.

00:52:53.870 --> 00:52:57.020
So, I wouldn't say, "Well,
we just gotta burn it down,

00:52:57.020 --> 00:52:59.950
do rehiring and don't put
anybody in the discipline."

00:52:59.950 --> 00:53:01.653
I'm certainly not saying that,

00:53:02.740 --> 00:53:04.420
but I just wanna illuminate

00:53:04.420 --> 00:53:06.820
that when we accept the categories,

00:53:06.820 --> 00:53:09.880
when we accept the disciplinary structures

00:53:09.880 --> 00:53:13.740
and we allow the power that
they hold over resources,

00:53:13.740 --> 00:53:14.950
there's no question.

00:53:14.950 --> 00:53:16.917
I don't think anybody on
our panel would say like

00:53:16.917 --> 00:53:21.917
"Our history department is in
any jeopardy of disappearing

00:53:22.130 --> 00:53:23.317
any time soon."

00:53:24.300 --> 00:53:25.970
Not to pick on historians,

00:53:25.970 --> 00:53:28.300
but I could say a number of disciplines.

00:53:28.300 --> 00:53:32.850
And that fact is a really
important part of the problem

00:53:32.850 --> 00:53:35.260
and the circumstance that we have.

00:53:35.260 --> 00:53:37.280
So, what I'm really asking us to do

00:53:37.280 --> 00:53:39.850
is be not so much radical only in action,

00:53:39.850 --> 00:53:43.390
but in thinking about knowledge production

00:53:43.390 --> 00:53:45.840
so that we don't end up here again.

00:53:45.840 --> 00:53:50.590
And by here, I mean that
amazing work is being done

00:53:50.590 --> 00:53:53.143
but as a number of people mentioned,

00:53:54.420 --> 00:53:56.360
there's fragility around getting tenure.

00:53:56.360 --> 00:53:58.450
There's fragility about
what kind of work matters.

00:53:58.450 --> 00:54:00.320
There's fragility around
what kinds of arguments

00:54:00.320 --> 00:54:01.390
you can make about race

00:54:01.390 --> 00:54:04.790
and expect to have the
same type of career.

00:54:04.790 --> 00:54:07.620
And all of those things
are part of the issue

00:54:07.620 --> 00:54:11.499
that brought our centers into
existence in the first place.

00:54:11.499 --> 00:54:13.900
So, you can't have it both ways.

00:54:13.900 --> 00:54:16.110
We can't have it both
ways, it seems to me.

00:54:16.110 --> 00:54:17.967
And I just wanna remind us of that

00:54:19.634 --> 00:54:22.510
as we think of how we want our centers

00:54:22.510 --> 00:54:24.230
to function going forward.

00:54:24.230 --> 00:54:25.480
- Wonderful, Tricia.

00:54:25.480 --> 00:54:26.327
I like your line,

00:54:26.327 --> 00:54:30.200
"Disciplines are products
of political skirmishes."

00:54:30.200 --> 00:54:31.233
That's a good line.

00:54:32.434 --> 00:54:34.800
- [Tricia] Copyright for
later, you can have it.

00:54:34.800 --> 00:54:37.147
- There's always a food fight happening.

00:54:38.752 --> 00:54:41.980
No, these are all wonderful comments.

00:54:41.980 --> 00:54:46.980
Now I'm going to go to
the prompt questions

00:54:46.980 --> 00:54:51.980
that I have here and the one
that fascinates me most is,

00:54:52.060 --> 00:54:55.050
actually, I'm doctoring one of them,

00:54:55.050 --> 00:54:59.253
which is what has been the success,

00:55:00.380 --> 00:55:05.080
or how do you think you
might operationalize

00:55:07.450 --> 00:55:10.910
placing race and equity studies

00:55:10.910 --> 00:55:14.120
on the entire undergraduate curriculum?

00:55:14.120 --> 00:55:18.170
So, everybody is interested,
has to do something about it.

00:55:18.170 --> 00:55:22.090
Do you think this is just utopia?

00:55:22.090 --> 00:55:26.060
Or at Princeton some years ago,

00:55:26.060 --> 00:55:31.060
they nominated Anthony Appiah's
book on cosmopolitanism

00:55:32.280 --> 00:55:36.813
that everyone entering Princeton
that year had to read it.

00:55:38.140 --> 00:55:41.890
Now Appiah's theory of cosmopolitanism

00:55:41.890 --> 00:55:43.990
is what one might describe

00:55:43.990 --> 00:55:47.710
as a gentleman's theory
of cosmopolitanism.

00:55:47.710 --> 00:55:50.070
It is not anything that would,

00:55:50.070 --> 00:55:52.430
I think, it doesn't
poke anyone in the eye.

00:55:52.430 --> 00:55:54.470
He's himself is such a gentleman.

00:55:54.470 --> 00:55:56.370
So, but that's what the students read.

00:55:56.370 --> 00:56:00.730
And so they all came out
feeling completely cosmopolitan

00:56:00.730 --> 00:56:05.730
but not in ways that would
unsettle the very principle

00:56:05.830 --> 00:56:08.540
of cosmopolitanism as such.

00:56:08.540 --> 00:56:13.540
So, my question is how might
we persuade our universities

00:56:15.830 --> 00:56:19.360
to make race and equity

00:56:19.360 --> 00:56:23.450
a general concern across the campus?

00:56:23.450 --> 00:56:24.793
So, that's my question.

00:56:26.998 --> 00:56:29.803
So, starting with Jennifer again.

00:56:31.050 --> 00:56:33.910
- Okay, and I hope we will
get to lots of questions too,

00:56:33.910 --> 00:56:35.710
if there's some from the audience,

00:56:35.710 --> 00:56:39.040
I guess I would just say that again,

00:56:39.040 --> 00:56:41.163
what does it mean to read?

00:56:42.760 --> 00:56:44.620
Because there are I think four books

00:56:44.620 --> 00:56:47.970
for the Stanford students this year,

00:56:47.970 --> 00:56:51.670
one of them, I think "Call Me By My Name."

00:56:51.670 --> 00:56:53.300
And they have over the years

00:56:53.300 --> 00:56:58.300
had Neil deGrasse Tyson's
work and so forth,

00:57:00.120 --> 00:57:05.120
but how are those texts
being read and interpreted,

00:57:06.520 --> 00:57:08.413
rejected or valued?

00:57:10.039 --> 00:57:14.163
This is then at the heart of how also,

00:57:15.600 --> 00:57:20.320
I think we need to transform
not just what we teach,

00:57:20.320 --> 00:57:23.450
to go back to your example
earlier of Shakespeare

00:57:23.450 --> 00:57:26.853
where you can open that up to
questions of race and equity,

00:57:28.090 --> 00:57:33.090
but it's also how you teach
and what that syllabi is,

00:57:33.230 --> 00:57:36.990
what's the method and what ultimately

00:57:36.990 --> 00:57:38.270
are you trying to change?

00:57:38.270 --> 00:57:43.270
And is it then also not
just about how we read

00:57:43.480 --> 00:57:46.400
or what we read, but in what context,

00:57:46.400 --> 00:57:50.560
and again, I'll just
say for whose benefit,

00:57:50.560 --> 00:57:51.800
because I think too often,

00:57:51.800 --> 00:57:55.020
even the way those books are selected,

00:57:55.020 --> 00:57:58.230
have to do with still the larger problem

00:57:59.200 --> 00:58:02.890
that we have inherited of
the university as a space,

00:58:02.890 --> 00:58:06.943
Oxbridge, of educating a liberal citizen,

00:58:08.538 --> 00:58:13.050
citizenry always already
self-segregated previously

00:58:13.050 --> 00:58:16.670
before we had EOC or ways of diversifying

00:58:16.670 --> 00:58:20.650
when schools had their
quota of Jewish professors

00:58:20.650 --> 00:58:23.070
or blacks like Dubois
were not allowed to teach

00:58:23.070 --> 00:58:24.763
at University of Pennsylvania.

00:58:25.810 --> 00:58:29.930
The very idea of the university student

00:58:31.540 --> 00:58:36.357
is one that I believe is
still imagined to be ideally

00:58:39.124 --> 00:58:42.340
a certain type of person.

00:58:42.340 --> 00:58:45.830
And so that whole idea of what
is the liberal education for,

00:58:45.830 --> 00:58:47.560
I mean, I don't wanna
go to metal with that,

00:58:47.560 --> 00:58:49.300
but I think it's relevant.

00:58:49.300 --> 00:58:52.350
Again, whom do we imagine we are serving

00:58:52.350 --> 00:58:55.510
and how those books would
even be interpreted?

00:58:55.510 --> 00:58:58.400
- And then also the
relationship between university

00:58:58.400 --> 00:59:02.650
and community, because
often universities are like

00:59:02.650 --> 00:59:04.480
they're elite pods.

00:59:04.480 --> 00:59:08.110
The elite pods of innovation
and some university department

00:59:08.110 --> 00:59:12.300
have no interest in what is
happening in the outside world.

00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:14.440
So, also the relationship
between what we do

00:59:14.440 --> 00:59:16.426
and the communities beyond.

00:59:16.426 --> 00:59:18.700
- That's why (indistinct)
has a model tradition

00:59:18.700 --> 00:59:20.830
of community engaged learning,

00:59:20.830 --> 00:59:23.000
and we have students do an internship,

00:59:23.000 --> 00:59:24.290
but of this particular kind

00:59:24.290 --> 00:59:26.010
where they're prepared
to think about questions

00:59:26.010 --> 00:59:29.670
of community-based research and solidarity

00:59:29.670 --> 00:59:34.597
before they even go out
into the "community spaces."

00:59:35.660 --> 00:59:40.022
- All right, so, Stephen, do
you have something to say?

00:59:40.022 --> 00:59:43.000
- I can answer this
question quickly, I think.

00:59:43.000 --> 00:59:46.550
How could we have these
conversations carefully?

00:59:46.550 --> 00:59:49.550
I mean, I think that that's a
really an important principle.

00:59:50.800 --> 00:59:53.280
Expertly, I think is another one.

00:59:53.280 --> 00:59:57.560
They can't be had on college
and university campuses

00:59:57.560 --> 01:00:02.560
and rambly in ways without careful thought

01:00:04.480 --> 01:00:06.647
and also in ways that are,

01:00:06.647 --> 01:00:08.210
and this is really important, I think,

01:00:08.210 --> 01:00:10.000
cognizant of the differential ways

01:00:10.000 --> 01:00:12.680
in which people enter and
experience these conversations.

01:00:12.680 --> 01:00:16.883
I mean, for so many of
our students they're,

01:00:18.339 --> 01:00:20.220
I mean, college is so different
from for most of them,

01:00:20.220 --> 01:00:21.610
for what they grew up with

01:00:21.610 --> 01:00:26.280
and those who we would
wanna be very cognizant

01:00:26.280 --> 01:00:27.730
of supporting in these conversations

01:00:27.730 --> 01:00:31.280
can oftentimes take these
conversations as very difficult

01:00:31.280 --> 01:00:32.420
and very hard.

01:00:32.420 --> 01:00:35.730
And the lack of support
of these conversations

01:00:35.730 --> 01:00:39.760
for people who experienced
these issues directly

01:00:39.760 --> 01:00:42.590
and disproportionately
can be very challenging.

01:00:42.590 --> 01:00:44.010
So, those would be at least just so,

01:00:44.010 --> 01:00:46.040
without trying to get too
much into the details,

01:00:46.040 --> 01:00:47.280
those would be at least three principles

01:00:47.280 --> 01:00:48.410
that I would wanna suggest

01:00:48.410 --> 01:00:51.980
ought to be guiding any discussion.

01:00:51.980 --> 01:00:54.043
- [Ato] Wonderful, Riley?

01:00:56.050 --> 01:01:01.050
- Thanks, and I am really just struck by,

01:01:01.320 --> 01:01:03.530
I think, what some of the great principles

01:01:03.530 --> 01:01:04.363
that have been put on the table

01:01:04.363 --> 01:01:07.370
and I'll add, I think just one more thing,

01:01:07.370 --> 01:01:11.110
which is really that,
and the conversation,

01:01:11.110 --> 01:01:12.950
intellectual conversations about race

01:01:12.950 --> 01:01:14.270
are happening in places

01:01:14.270 --> 01:01:16.940
in which they are also
being actively disavowed.

01:01:16.940 --> 01:01:21.940
And so it's also about
providing a way of seeing

01:01:22.370 --> 01:01:24.410
that what we are doing is engaging

01:01:24.410 --> 01:01:29.410
in racialized forms of thought
that may be absented of,

01:01:30.200 --> 01:01:33.550
what we see as a legible
understanding of a racialized body

01:01:33.550 --> 01:01:37.330
or how race plays out
in terms of discourses

01:01:37.330 --> 01:01:38.230
of race relations.

01:01:41.980 --> 01:01:43.870
- [Ato] All right, Tricia

01:01:43.870 --> 01:01:45.283
and then we'll open it
up to the panelists.

01:01:45.283 --> 01:01:46.772
- Yeah, yeah, sure.

01:01:46.772 --> 01:01:48.343
Super, super quick.

01:01:48.343 --> 01:01:52.410
I think the way I would encourage us to do

01:01:52.410 --> 01:01:55.950
critical race studies
intervention in the curriculum

01:01:55.950 --> 01:01:57.550
is to remind our students

01:01:57.550 --> 01:02:01.130
and the disciplines to remind themselves

01:02:01.130 --> 01:02:05.570
that they were formed
under a white supremacist,

01:02:05.570 --> 01:02:09.033
colonialist infrastructure
and ideological framework.

01:02:10.070 --> 01:02:11.290
All of them.

01:02:11.290 --> 01:02:14.130
And that that's not a sub piece

01:02:14.130 --> 01:02:15.910
of the puzzle off on the side.

01:02:15.910 --> 01:02:19.500
It was a fundamental
component, not the only one.

01:02:19.500 --> 01:02:23.130
Not even necessarily the super
most, most important one,

01:02:23.130 --> 01:02:25.400
although I think it ranks up there

01:02:25.400 --> 01:02:29.940
and that that as a mode
of self interrogation,

01:02:29.940 --> 01:02:32.290
you have to be willing
to interrogate the self

01:02:32.290 --> 01:02:35.610
that's a Western philosophical framework

01:02:35.610 --> 01:02:37.280
from Socrates in the first place,

01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:39.660
you must interrogate the self

01:02:39.660 --> 01:02:43.020
in order to grow, to learn, to transform.

01:02:43.020 --> 01:02:45.980
So, it wouldn't just be about
adding courses or readings,

01:02:45.980 --> 01:02:47.520
it's about asking the disciplines

01:02:47.520 --> 01:02:51.700
to do the true self
interrogation that's necessary

01:02:51.700 --> 01:02:55.300
to understand why race
is a central element

01:02:55.300 --> 01:02:57.010
in the formation of everything

01:02:57.010 --> 01:02:58.810
in the ways in which these ideas

01:02:58.810 --> 01:03:01.770
that were not about race
on their explicit terms,

01:03:01.770 --> 01:03:04.240
were all about race and control and space

01:03:04.240 --> 01:03:05.300
and who was a problem

01:03:05.300 --> 01:03:07.810
and who needed to be
excluded from governance

01:03:07.810 --> 01:03:08.810
and who didn't,

01:03:08.810 --> 01:03:11.330
what mattered in the
constitution and what didn't,

01:03:11.330 --> 01:03:12.407
so on and so forth.

01:03:12.407 --> 01:03:14.780
And the principles were great,
we just added black people.

01:03:14.780 --> 01:03:17.300
We messed up the first
time, we added them later.

01:03:17.300 --> 01:03:18.990
No, no, that's not what happened.

01:03:18.990 --> 01:03:22.790
So, that's what I think is
more important than anything

01:03:22.790 --> 01:03:27.110
in terms of really to making
the change that we want.

01:03:27.110 --> 01:03:28.000
- Wonderful to hear.

01:03:28.000 --> 01:03:29.450
Wonderful, you all.

01:03:29.450 --> 01:03:34.230
So, now the questions,
are we going to Meghan?

01:03:34.230 --> 01:03:38.310
Are you going to read them
out for us or should I?

01:03:39.240 --> 01:03:40.210
- [Stephen] I can do that.

01:03:40.210 --> 01:03:42.077
- You'll do, all right, please.

01:03:42.077 --> 01:03:44.470
- Let me pose this question to all of us.

01:03:44.470 --> 01:03:47.850
We have a question from
this app from an instructor,

01:03:47.850 --> 01:03:51.590
I think who is asking a general question

01:03:51.590 --> 01:03:53.930
about requirements in their fields.

01:03:53.930 --> 01:03:56.880
And the specifics are
about language requirements

01:03:59.050 --> 01:04:01.800
that are required for the
achievement of a degree

01:04:01.800 --> 01:04:04.870
that strike this person
as perhaps outdated

01:04:04.870 --> 01:04:09.870
and potentially a mechanism
for predetermining

01:04:10.460 --> 01:04:13.240
the direction of the discipline, I think.

01:04:13.240 --> 01:04:16.690
So maybe what the question
is on the table is,

01:04:16.690 --> 01:04:19.180
how should people be
thinking about these things

01:04:19.180 --> 01:04:21.880
that may be ostensibly race neutral

01:04:21.880 --> 01:04:24.340
in a particular discipline or department

01:04:24.340 --> 01:04:27.580
that actually do have the
effects of perpetuating

01:04:27.580 --> 01:04:31.190
ways of approaching topics
that either mischaracterize

01:04:31.190 --> 01:04:33.930
or fail, even to recognize the
topics we're talking about.

01:04:33.930 --> 01:04:37.490
So, I just asked the question,
so I'm not gonna answer it.

01:04:37.490 --> 01:04:39.110
I guess I'll turn it to any one of you

01:04:39.110 --> 01:04:40.360
who wants to take it off.

01:04:42.760 --> 01:04:45.360
- Well, I guess maybe I'll
just say super quickly

01:04:45.360 --> 01:04:47.260
that when I was in graduate school,

01:04:47.260 --> 01:04:50.550
one of my colleagues petitioned
to have Jamaican Creole,

01:04:50.550 --> 01:04:53.470
which she was studying Caribbean count

01:04:53.470 --> 01:04:56.150
for her "foreign language."

01:04:56.150 --> 01:04:58.820
She herself was actually West Indian

01:04:59.760 --> 01:05:04.760
and so these questions of,
again, the scholarly languages,

01:05:05.810 --> 01:05:07.347
I'm not saying don't know them,

01:05:07.347 --> 01:05:10.220
but they have that tradition that Tricia

01:05:10.220 --> 01:05:12.850
just laid out for us so nicely,

01:05:12.850 --> 01:05:15.310
again, of what are the languages to know.

01:05:15.310 --> 01:05:19.773
And those two came us out
of a particular hierarchy

01:05:20.720 --> 01:05:22.700
or where we thought scholarly thought

01:05:22.700 --> 01:05:25.313
and in what language
scholarly thought happens.

01:05:28.683 --> 01:05:29.516
- Thank you.

01:05:29.516 --> 01:05:32.860
I think the matter of language acquisition

01:05:32.860 --> 01:05:35.200
and Jennifer, your
comments really got me here

01:05:35.200 --> 01:05:38.450
is that really it's supposed
to signify one of the tools

01:05:38.450 --> 01:05:40.050
that's needed to get at the thing

01:05:40.050 --> 01:05:44.290
that you want to get at inside
of your particular training.

01:05:44.290 --> 01:05:47.380
And so I also was
trained in media studies,

01:05:47.380 --> 01:05:51.430
a fairly new 20th century
invention of a discipline

01:05:51.430 --> 01:05:54.220
wherein things like knowing code

01:05:54.220 --> 01:05:59.220
was a way to suffice in
your language requirements.

01:05:59.230 --> 01:06:03.160
So, I think these kinds
of questions as you put it

01:06:03.160 --> 01:06:06.220
that are seemingly race neutral,

01:06:06.220 --> 01:06:10.470
but also get at a larger
philosophical orientation

01:06:10.470 --> 01:06:12.480
of what it means to be graduate curriculum

01:06:12.480 --> 01:06:16.540
in which the case has to be made why X

01:06:16.540 --> 01:06:19.320
in the service of what
you are aiming to do

01:06:20.220 --> 01:06:21.740
both in terms of your question,

01:06:21.740 --> 01:06:24.130
but also in terms of the
container of the program

01:06:24.130 --> 01:06:27.013
that you happen to be sitting
in while you do your work now.

01:06:29.190 --> 01:06:31.020
- Ato, do you wanna take a shot at this?

01:06:31.020 --> 01:06:32.278
I mean, you may-

01:06:32.278 --> 01:06:33.593
- Don't have anything to add.

01:06:34.710 --> 01:06:36.327
- Steve, you go ahead too.

01:06:37.639 --> 01:06:41.352
- Please go ahead with the questions.

01:06:41.352 --> 01:06:43.693
- I wonder if this also
relates to another question

01:06:43.693 --> 01:06:44.526
that was asked,

01:06:44.526 --> 01:06:47.950
which is essentially what
students might be doing

01:06:47.950 --> 01:06:51.130
if they're working in a
field, in a department,

01:06:51.130 --> 01:06:53.550
in a so-called discipline
in which the issues

01:06:53.550 --> 01:06:55.750
that we've been talking
about centering race

01:06:56.810 --> 01:06:58.570
haven't really made it to their fore.

01:06:58.570 --> 01:07:00.920
Any thoughts for any
students in the audience

01:07:00.920 --> 01:07:04.740
who may be struggling with
these very specific questions

01:07:04.740 --> 01:07:06.170
in their own departmental home?

01:07:06.170 --> 01:07:08.603
Whether those are undergraduates
or graduate students?

01:07:09.470 --> 01:07:11.490
- Can I jump in?
- Yeah, yeah.

01:07:11.490 --> 01:07:14.830
- At Toronto, I run
the Center for Diaspora

01:07:14.830 --> 01:07:16.230
and Transnational Studies.

01:07:16.230 --> 01:07:19.960
A new, so, we set it up,
it's now like 15 years old.

01:07:19.960 --> 01:07:22.420
And so outside the University of Toronto,

01:07:22.420 --> 01:07:25.550
before I moved, I jumped around
and could go to Stanford.

01:07:25.550 --> 01:07:27.457
And this question came up all the time,

01:07:27.457 --> 01:07:29.020
"Why diaspora studies?"

01:07:29.020 --> 01:07:31.250
And one of the things that
I pointed out to my students

01:07:31.250 --> 01:07:36.250
is that every respectable
institution these days

01:07:36.400 --> 01:07:41.400
has diversity, they'll
call it different things.

01:07:42.260 --> 01:07:46.420
They have a diversity office

01:07:46.420 --> 01:07:48.700
and they're interested in diversity.

01:07:48.700 --> 01:07:50.810
So, that for example, banks,

01:07:50.810 --> 01:07:55.263
many banks have found out
that as new immigrants arrive

01:07:56.310 --> 01:07:59.350
and settle, new immigrants
are very interested

01:07:59.350 --> 01:08:02.460
in putting down roots by buying homes.

01:08:02.460 --> 01:08:06.400
So, every bank has a diversity officer

01:08:06.400 --> 01:08:11.380
whose interest is in
targeting marketing products

01:08:11.380 --> 01:08:14.587
to newly arrived immigrants

01:08:14.587 --> 01:08:17.210
and some banks are more
successful than that.

01:08:17.210 --> 01:08:21.170
But as someone who has
done critical risk studies

01:08:21.170 --> 01:08:25.730
cannot only shape the
direction of these products,

01:08:25.730 --> 01:08:30.200
but also give the bank a
higher as it were consciousness

01:08:30.200 --> 01:08:31.680
on these things.

01:08:31.680 --> 01:08:34.920
Let me jump to another completely
ridiculous example, FIFA,

01:08:34.920 --> 01:08:39.760
the football Federation
is very much about,

01:08:39.760 --> 01:08:43.060
they say so themselves, so we
can take them on their word,

01:08:43.060 --> 01:08:48.060
equity, the distribution of
resources to various leagues,

01:08:48.650 --> 01:08:53.410
to historically marginalized
populations and so on.

01:08:53.410 --> 01:08:57.250
What better than someone who
has done critical race studies

01:08:57.250 --> 01:08:59.340
to go and work at FIFA and tell them

01:08:59.340 --> 01:09:01.860
that you're looking at the
thing completely wrong,

01:09:01.860 --> 01:09:05.380
and from my tree, in other
words, there are many,

01:09:05.380 --> 01:09:10.380
and the more we produce
students who are savvy

01:09:10.660 --> 01:09:13.400
and understanding of critical race studies

01:09:13.400 --> 01:09:17.190
and the more impact that they
make in different industries.

01:09:17.190 --> 01:09:21.773
And so on, the more value
will be given to our fields.

01:09:22.690 --> 01:09:25.430
So, this is one answer that
I used to give to my students

01:09:25.430 --> 01:09:27.460
in Diaspora and Transnational Studies.

01:09:27.460 --> 01:09:30.230
They need you, just tell them you are here

01:09:30.230 --> 01:09:33.760
and they will hire you.

01:09:33.760 --> 01:09:36.947
- Yeah, I'm wondering if I
could jump in for a second, Ato,

01:09:36.947 --> 01:09:40.070
and just ask some of my colleagues here

01:09:40.070 --> 01:09:45.070
a couple of questions
about what they imagine

01:09:45.750 --> 01:09:49.700
would be some important
new areas to move in

01:09:49.700 --> 01:09:53.060
in terms of their centers
or things they have on tap

01:09:53.060 --> 01:09:55.623
for the people who joined us today to say,

01:09:56.687 --> 01:09:59.817
"What are we gonna do with
the space that we have?"

01:10:00.670 --> 01:10:02.450
Maybe it's programming for next year,

01:10:02.450 --> 01:10:05.240
maybe it's envisioning something
five years down the road,

01:10:05.240 --> 01:10:09.020
maybe it's challenges, whatever it is,

01:10:09.020 --> 01:10:12.000
but I think, what are we going to do

01:10:12.000 --> 01:10:13.760
that we're either excited about,

01:10:13.760 --> 01:10:15.880
that we think is going to change

01:10:16.770 --> 01:10:18.970
the landscape of where we are

01:10:18.970 --> 01:10:21.310
or what kinds of things
do you wish you could do

01:10:21.310 --> 01:10:23.260
and do you just can't
see your way right now?

01:10:23.260 --> 01:10:27.180
But I think it's important
to imagine a future together

01:10:27.180 --> 01:10:30.730
and anyone, Jennifer, Riley, Stephen?

01:10:30.730 --> 01:10:31.623
- I can jump in.

01:10:32.970 --> 01:10:34.750
This is an opportunity
to say how excited I am

01:10:34.750 --> 01:10:37.440
to be working with the colleagues here

01:10:37.440 --> 01:10:39.550
on this centering race consortium

01:10:40.800 --> 01:10:42.230
that was funded by the Mellon Foundation.

01:10:42.230 --> 01:10:44.750
So, one thing I would say very positively

01:10:44.750 --> 01:10:47.120
that I think we're really
excited about doing

01:10:47.120 --> 01:10:49.550
is looking for ways to
partner with other colleges

01:10:49.550 --> 01:10:53.660
and universities to expand
the sense of community,

01:10:53.660 --> 01:10:58.430
the good ideas, the
opportunities to think together

01:10:58.430 --> 01:11:00.260
about issues that are just so critical,

01:11:00.260 --> 01:11:02.070
that we all agree are critical.

01:11:02.070 --> 01:11:05.260
There's just not enough of us

01:11:05.260 --> 01:11:06.960
to go around on any given campus,

01:11:06.960 --> 01:11:10.350
and we need one another's
support and good ideas.

01:11:10.350 --> 01:11:12.850
We need to be able to create
opportunities for one another.

01:11:12.850 --> 01:11:14.840
We need critical feedback from one another

01:11:14.840 --> 01:11:17.470
that we may not get on our home campuses.

01:11:17.470 --> 01:11:21.610
So, the chance to work with
the colleagues on this call

01:11:21.610 --> 01:11:25.490
and with others around, on these campuses

01:11:25.490 --> 01:11:27.490
is really exciting to me.

01:11:27.490 --> 01:11:30.490
And we're hoping to be able to do this

01:11:30.490 --> 01:11:32.620
with faculty involved,
with students involved,

01:11:32.620 --> 01:11:34.407
with artists residents involved,

01:11:34.407 --> 01:11:37.090
and so I'm really excited about that.

01:11:37.090 --> 01:11:38.660
One thing that I'm concerned about,

01:11:38.660 --> 01:11:40.862
and I don't know if any of the rest of you

01:11:40.862 --> 01:11:45.862
struggle with this is that art
center has gotten pulled in

01:11:45.920 --> 01:11:49.450
on our campus to institutional efforts

01:11:49.450 --> 01:11:53.070
related to diversity equity and belonging.

01:11:53.070 --> 01:11:58.070
The DEID efforts and some
ways we wanna be there,

01:11:58.170 --> 01:12:02.211
because I do think that the
intellectual work that we do,

01:12:02.211 --> 01:12:07.211
the credentialing and amplifying of work

01:12:07.230 --> 01:12:10.890
in these areas as critical
areas of research and teaching

01:12:10.890 --> 01:12:13.700
does translate to questions of climate.

01:12:13.700 --> 01:12:17.500
There's a relationship
between ennobling these fields

01:12:17.500 --> 01:12:21.020
and who gets hired and who
stays on a given campus.

01:12:21.020 --> 01:12:22.380
So, there's reasons why

01:12:22.380 --> 01:12:25.490
we wanna be involved in this work.

01:12:25.490 --> 01:12:28.960
At the same time, there
are sets of challenges,

01:12:28.960 --> 01:12:32.450
I think here when academics like me

01:12:32.450 --> 01:12:34.410
and I think like all of us

01:12:34.410 --> 01:12:35.940
get pulled into these conversations,

01:12:35.940 --> 01:12:39.810
into committees about
tone setting on campus,

01:12:39.810 --> 01:12:44.240
best practices on campus
that are not related

01:12:44.240 --> 01:12:46.640
to the research and
teaching and other things

01:12:46.640 --> 01:12:48.890
that I think we're primarily focused on.

01:12:48.890 --> 01:12:53.510
So, I'm just flagging that as
an area of challenge for us.

01:12:53.510 --> 01:12:55.890
And I didn't know if anybody else

01:12:55.890 --> 01:12:57.120
had any thoughts about that.

01:12:57.120 --> 01:12:59.960
So, I've given you the positive,
which is the consortium,

01:12:59.960 --> 01:13:02.250
that's one thing and then
I'm raising the question

01:13:02.250 --> 01:13:06.040
of if and how our centers
have a role to play

01:13:06.040 --> 01:13:07.640
in these broader university efforts.

01:13:07.640 --> 01:13:10.400
I don't wanna be an HR
specialist, for example,

01:13:10.400 --> 01:13:12.970
and I feel sometimes like I
do get pulled into that stuff

01:13:12.970 --> 01:13:15.970
and our center does and if
we get pulled into that,

01:13:15.970 --> 01:13:18.180
we're not doing other important
work that we could be doing.

01:13:18.180 --> 01:13:20.510
So, if that's of interest,

01:13:20.510 --> 01:13:22.510
maybe somebody wants to comment on that.

01:13:23.490 --> 01:13:26.410
- Jennifer or Riley, anybody?

01:13:26.410 --> 01:13:30.330
- Well, the thing is
that one of the reasons

01:13:30.330 --> 01:13:34.060
why Stanford, I'll have to
talk about Stanford, Jennifer,

01:13:34.060 --> 01:13:35.160
and correct me if I'm
wrong, 'cause I'm new.

01:13:35.160 --> 01:13:38.450
I'm only two years at Stanford.

01:13:38.450 --> 01:13:43.450
Why the recruitment and
retention of faculty of color

01:13:43.750 --> 01:13:47.120
has been a problem is
specifically because,

01:13:47.120 --> 01:13:49.860
not just because of the tone, Stephen,

01:13:49.860 --> 01:13:51.770
what you just mentioned, the tone,

01:13:51.770 --> 01:13:54.210
but also because for some reason,

01:13:54.210 --> 01:13:57.530
the numbers don't, they
never are able to grow

01:13:57.530 --> 01:14:00.103
because people come and then they leave.

01:14:01.080 --> 01:14:05.170
So, our being pulled into these things,

01:14:05.170 --> 01:14:07.010
distracting though they might seem,

01:14:07.010 --> 01:14:08.870
sometimes almost inescapable,

01:14:08.870 --> 01:14:12.310
because we want to increase the density

01:14:12.310 --> 01:14:17.230
of faculty of color, but
to increase the density

01:14:17.230 --> 01:14:20.310
and the numbers, we have
to somehow contribute

01:14:20.310 --> 01:14:22.810
to creating the environment

01:14:22.810 --> 01:14:27.580
where the faculty of color
arriving sees themselves in it.

01:14:27.580 --> 01:14:31.370
This is especially crucial
for pre-tenure faculty.

01:14:31.370 --> 01:14:34.150
So, senior faculty like
me, I'm at home everywhere,

01:14:34.150 --> 01:14:35.420
it doesn't really matter,

01:14:35.420 --> 01:14:40.030
but I can imagine if I had
arrived as a pre-tenure,

01:14:40.030 --> 01:14:43.360
I would have been scared for
the rest of my seven years

01:14:43.360 --> 01:14:45.380
before I got tenure at Stanford,

01:14:45.380 --> 01:14:50.380
because the history of the
tenuring of a faculty of color,

01:14:51.700 --> 01:14:53.650
let's assume the English department

01:14:54.660 --> 01:14:58.680
let's put it very
diplomatically, it's sub optimal,

01:14:58.680 --> 01:15:00.800
and this is putting it diplomatically.

01:15:00.800 --> 01:15:05.183
So, in a way, you almost cannot escape it.

01:15:06.230 --> 01:15:08.930
- I think you've put
it in managerial terms

01:15:08.930 --> 01:15:11.410
and that was my gesture
towards the global university,

01:15:11.410 --> 01:15:14.480
but like University of Chicago,

01:15:14.480 --> 01:15:17.820
we at Stanford suffer from
not having a department,

01:15:17.820 --> 01:15:20.100
for example, of African and
African-American studies

01:15:20.100 --> 01:15:22.470
before I too was hired as a full professor

01:15:22.470 --> 01:15:24.670
over 10 years ago at Stanford,

01:15:24.670 --> 01:15:26.030
I was a full professor

01:15:26.030 --> 01:15:29.280
in African and
African-American studies 100%.

01:15:29.280 --> 01:15:32.013
So, the kinds of reach
and interdisciplinarity

01:15:32.013 --> 01:15:34.770
and centering race that we've talked about

01:15:34.770 --> 01:15:39.770
was very much a given and
understood and valued.

01:15:41.070 --> 01:15:44.800
And we are like University
of Chicago, fighting

01:15:44.800 --> 01:15:45.980
and as you well know,

01:15:45.980 --> 01:15:48.440
Ato, 'cause you've been
a wonderful part of that.

01:15:48.440 --> 01:15:50.040
Not only to have a department

01:15:50.040 --> 01:15:51.820
and maybe a department of ethnic studies,

01:15:51.820 --> 01:15:54.380
I don't know what the
landscape will look like,

01:15:54.380 --> 01:15:57.090
but also an Institute
because it's at Stanford

01:15:57.090 --> 01:16:01.240
that institutes have great value.

01:16:01.240 --> 01:16:05.350
And literally in terms
of resources and scope

01:16:05.350 --> 01:16:08.360
and hiring more people
so it's not the same few.

01:16:08.360 --> 01:16:10.340
the numbers of black faculty at Stanford

01:16:10.340 --> 01:16:13.253
have remained statistically
flat since the 1980s.

01:16:15.700 --> 01:16:17.020
And I think that is in part

01:16:17.020 --> 01:16:20.580
because we've invested in fact
too much in junior people,

01:16:20.580 --> 01:16:22.140
which is continuing to happen,

01:16:22.140 --> 01:16:24.220
who are brilliant and wonderful,

01:16:24.220 --> 01:16:27.010
but don't necessarily have the wherewithal

01:16:27.010 --> 01:16:31.160
to compartmentalize and know the landscape

01:16:31.160 --> 01:16:34.570
or understand even necessarily
what a university is about.

01:16:34.570 --> 01:16:36.240
That's a huge transition

01:16:36.240 --> 01:16:39.770
and we're not taught in
graduate school to do this DEI

01:16:39.770 --> 01:16:41.363
or DEP work.

01:16:42.300 --> 01:16:44.850
And so it's, again, the
burdens of representation

01:16:44.850 --> 01:16:49.360
that fall too often on the
bodies that always already

01:16:49.360 --> 01:16:54.360
are laboring at a certain
kind of personal expense.

01:16:54.680 --> 01:16:58.199
So, we need to do more
of that structural change

01:16:58.199 --> 01:17:01.090
that everyone on the
call was talking about

01:17:01.090 --> 01:17:04.790
to really make it better labor conditions

01:17:04.790 --> 01:17:07.613
and really change the
face of the university.

01:17:10.110 --> 01:17:11.560
- If I could jump in, Riley,

01:17:11.560 --> 01:17:14.250
unless you were about to jump in.

01:17:14.250 --> 01:17:15.083
- Go ahead, Tricia.

01:17:15.083 --> 01:17:15.916
- Oh, okay.

01:17:15.916 --> 01:17:18.900
I just wanted to piggyback
for a second because I just,

01:17:18.900 --> 01:17:20.530
my favorite thing about Zoom

01:17:20.530 --> 01:17:23.620
is that you can like
quickly surf the internet

01:17:23.620 --> 01:17:25.530
before you say something.

01:17:25.530 --> 01:17:28.840
So, I wanna say that
Stanford's black faculty

01:17:28.840 --> 01:17:30.823
are 2% of the faculty.

01:17:32.010 --> 01:17:34.620
So, let's just think about the
numbers we're talking about.

01:17:34.620 --> 01:17:36.700
So, when we're saying, "Well, they leave,"

01:17:36.700 --> 01:17:37.970
it doesn't actually matter.

01:17:37.970 --> 01:17:40.990
You can't fix the climate with 2%.

01:17:40.990 --> 01:17:43.190
- And for black women, it's 1%.

01:17:43.190 --> 01:17:44.917
- Oh great, it's 1%.

01:17:44.917 --> 01:17:45.933
Well, the internet needs to go catch up.

01:17:45.933 --> 01:17:49.360
That's why I acknowledged I
went to the Stanford website.

01:17:49.360 --> 01:17:50.880
So that in case it was incorrect,

01:17:50.880 --> 01:17:53.120
I wasn't gonna be held responsible.

01:17:53.120 --> 01:17:57.790
But that's an unbelievable
failure in numbers.

01:17:57.790 --> 01:18:00.760
It's easy for me to say that
'cause I'm not in Stanford,

01:18:00.760 --> 01:18:03.870
but this is extremely important.

01:18:03.870 --> 01:18:05.610
Of course there's gonna be climate issues,

01:18:05.610 --> 01:18:07.977
I'm sure when they're
calling Stephen to say,

01:18:07.977 --> 01:18:08.980
"Can you discuss it?"

01:18:08.980 --> 01:18:11.800
It's all about we can't
afford to lose anymore

01:18:11.800 --> 01:18:16.800
or whatever the problem may
be, but this is a real problem.

01:18:18.580 --> 01:18:20.107
I mean, like we just can't stand around,

01:18:20.107 --> 01:18:21.930
act like we're all doing all of this

01:18:21.930 --> 01:18:26.790
and everyone isn't doing
race work either necessarily.

01:18:26.790 --> 01:18:29.210
So, when you bring those numbers down,

01:18:29.210 --> 01:18:31.633
you have to bring them down even further.

01:18:32.870 --> 01:18:33.750
And I don't think,

01:18:33.750 --> 01:18:36.600
I just wanna comment on
Steve's really important point

01:18:36.600 --> 01:18:38.570
about diversity work,

01:18:38.570 --> 01:18:41.350
that climate stuff is the university's job

01:18:41.350 --> 01:18:44.350
and there are lots of talented people

01:18:44.350 --> 01:18:47.030
who do this for a living and
they need to be invested in

01:18:47.030 --> 01:18:50.620
and all the faculty and staff
need to be participating

01:18:50.620 --> 01:18:52.340
in what to do with that.

01:18:52.340 --> 01:18:55.040
Certainly there's some
intellectual work that overlaps

01:18:55.040 --> 01:18:56.060
and dovetails with that

01:18:56.060 --> 01:18:59.290
and I get called to do the same
thing you're talking about.

01:18:59.290 --> 01:19:02.610
Some of my own research on
systemic racism overlaps.

01:19:02.610 --> 01:19:04.520
So, I can just stay, do my research

01:19:04.520 --> 01:19:06.873
and it still has a Diablo effect,

01:19:07.790 --> 01:19:09.870
but if not what you're doing,

01:19:09.870 --> 01:19:11.520
you shouldn't be called in there.

01:19:12.620 --> 01:19:14.440
But just one last thing on the positive,

01:19:14.440 --> 01:19:16.240
'cause I love Steve is positive, negative,

01:19:16.240 --> 01:19:17.530
I have to end with the positive

01:19:17.530 --> 01:19:21.760
'cause my negatives outweigh
the positives all the time.

01:19:21.760 --> 01:19:23.540
So my positive is not only

01:19:23.540 --> 01:19:26.420
that this has been super
exciting for the four schools

01:19:26.420 --> 01:19:27.253
to think this way,

01:19:27.253 --> 01:19:29.730
but that we've really been empowered

01:19:29.730 --> 01:19:33.550
to create cross-university curriculum

01:19:33.550 --> 01:19:35.730
and cross university teaching.

01:19:35.730 --> 01:19:39.300
And I think we should,
I'm empowered and inspired

01:19:39.300 --> 01:19:41.637
and our grant empowers
us to really do that.

01:19:41.637 --> 01:19:43.500
And I think that's super important

01:19:43.500 --> 01:19:47.680
because not only will that
help our various one to 2%

01:19:47.680 --> 01:19:49.010
on being community,

01:19:49.010 --> 01:19:54.010
but it will also just create
a collective knowledge

01:19:54.730 --> 01:19:57.450
and expand all of our
work much more quickly.

01:19:57.450 --> 01:20:01.250
So, this is Zoom, the sort
of crisis of the pandemic,

01:20:01.250 --> 01:20:04.220
creating a virtual world as the norm

01:20:04.220 --> 01:20:05.800
and the context of the grant

01:20:05.800 --> 01:20:07.840
and the geniuses who
were speaking yesterday

01:20:07.840 --> 01:20:10.480
and those of you who were
in the audience here,

01:20:10.480 --> 01:20:12.530
this is a really exciting moment

01:20:12.530 --> 01:20:17.220
where we can take this work,
make it public, make it share

01:20:17.220 --> 01:20:18.113
and push forward.

01:20:19.150 --> 01:20:21.003
- Let me say something
else that's exciting.

01:20:22.616 --> 01:20:23.449
I made this reference earlier

01:20:23.449 --> 01:20:25.193
to the politics of small numbers.

01:20:28.704 --> 01:20:30.623
This is the flip side of that 1%

01:20:30.623 --> 01:20:32.040
that we're talking about at Stanford,

01:20:32.040 --> 01:20:33.340
or I often say like,

01:20:33.340 --> 01:20:35.210
I'm one of three Mexican
American faculty members

01:20:35.210 --> 01:20:38.550
I know at Yale and I'm
married to the number two.

01:20:38.550 --> 01:20:41.330
And so there's only one
who I'm not married to

01:20:41.330 --> 01:20:42.163
on our campus.

01:20:42.163 --> 01:20:43.430
So, anyway, so small numbers.

01:20:44.640 --> 01:20:46.870
But the flip side of
that is what we do know

01:20:46.870 --> 01:20:49.850
is that in working with
students, with colleagues,

01:20:49.850 --> 01:20:53.130
if we can make differences in their lives,

01:20:53.130 --> 01:20:56.260
if we can help to bring
that one extra person

01:20:56.260 --> 01:20:59.320
to our campus or help them to
get that seminar paper done

01:20:59.320 --> 01:21:00.530
or get that master's thesis

01:21:00.530 --> 01:21:02.300
or that doctoral dissertation done,

01:21:02.300 --> 01:21:06.270
keep that colleague who
was hired on our campus

01:21:06.270 --> 01:21:07.940
but was considering a move,

01:21:07.940 --> 01:21:11.290
if we can make a positive
addition to any of those moments

01:21:11.290 --> 01:21:12.630
and those people's lives,

01:21:12.630 --> 01:21:16.370
this can have remarkable
effects on our fields,

01:21:16.370 --> 01:21:19.160
on the departments or units
in which they're working,

01:21:19.160 --> 01:21:22.430
on the students that they're
working alongside with,

01:21:22.430 --> 01:21:23.640
whether we're talking about a student

01:21:23.640 --> 01:21:27.433
or we're talking about a faculty member.

01:21:28.480 --> 01:21:31.000
We work with far more than
small numbers in our centers,

01:21:31.000 --> 01:21:31.930
that's certainly true.

01:21:31.930 --> 01:21:35.160
But when we succeed individual
by individual by individual,

01:21:35.160 --> 01:21:40.160
I think we can have really
important multiplying effects

01:21:41.540 --> 01:21:43.440
that ought to be recognized.

01:21:43.440 --> 01:21:46.490
And so that's one of the
things that makes me happy

01:21:46.490 --> 01:21:49.520
because it's in this work,

01:21:49.520 --> 01:21:51.710
there are moments where you feel set back

01:21:51.710 --> 01:21:54.340
by the larger atmosphere
in which we're working.

01:21:54.340 --> 01:21:57.290
I could have told stories about that

01:21:57.290 --> 01:21:58.900
as I know everybody on
this talk could have,

01:21:58.900 --> 01:22:01.730
and yet at the same time,
we are able to hold onto,

01:22:01.730 --> 01:22:06.730
I think these experiences
with small groups of people

01:22:07.230 --> 01:22:08.870
or even with individuals that we know

01:22:08.870 --> 01:22:10.500
really do have made a difference

01:22:10.500 --> 01:22:14.880
and just looking at you,
Jennifer, Riley, and Tricia,

01:22:14.880 --> 01:22:17.650
knowing some of the
faculty you've supported,

01:22:17.650 --> 01:22:19.430
the students you've supported,

01:22:19.430 --> 01:22:21.120
it's amazing to see the
work that you've done

01:22:21.120 --> 01:22:22.350
with those people over time

01:22:22.350 --> 01:22:24.653
and the way in which
your investment of time,

01:22:25.630 --> 01:22:29.020
your trust in their projects,
the leadership you've given

01:22:29.020 --> 01:22:31.940
really has made a really
important difference

01:22:31.940 --> 01:22:34.900
to people on your campuses, but
also well off your campuses.

01:22:34.900 --> 01:22:38.050
- I just saw a question here,
Riley, sorry to interrupt,

01:22:38.050 --> 01:22:39.400
but it's an important one, I think.

01:22:39.400 --> 01:22:43.627
It's from one of the participants.

01:22:43.627 --> 01:22:48.210
"Is the consortium open
to HBCU collaborations?"

01:22:48.210 --> 01:22:50.010
And this is a question for you guys.

